// Gap Diagnostic 2 (T008): the diagnostic rebuilt from real past papers and weighted to Sihan's measured weak areas.
// Split from results/gap-analysis (39 misses over RML 2020, IHBAS 2024, the JSS PYQ mock, MET Mock 1) and his 14 Oct topic ratings.
// Nothing from a paper he has sat (RML 2020, IHBAS 2024/25, JSS PYQ + Mock 2, St John's Mock 1 + 2, MET Mock 1, SGT).
// Cards reuse the checked concept, traps, topic and Key figure of their live source mock; keys re-solved blind by 2 checkers (60/60).
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS = window.PREVIEW_MOCKS || {};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["diagnostic-2"] = {"id": "diagnostic-2", "title": "Gap Diagnostic 2", "fullTitle": "Gap Diagnostic 2: your weak areas (60 real PYQs)", "sub": "60 real past-paper questions · weighted to the subjects your misses come from · sit it once · results by subject", "sectionLabels": {"general": "General and Cognitive Psychology", "assessment": "Psychological Assessment", "social": "Social Psychology", "personality": "Personality", "physio": "Biopsychology", "dev": "Developmental Psychology", "clinical": "Clinical Psychology and Therapy", "disorders": "Psychopathology", "research": "Research Methods and Statistics"}, "questions": [{"q": "Who gave the concept of ‘Folk psychology’?", "options": {"a": "Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt", "b": "Sigmund Freud", "c": "Ivan Pavlov", "d": "Wilhelm Roentgen"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "The correct answer is Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt is famous as the founder of experimental psychology, having established the first formal psychology laboratory in Leipzig in 1879, and for trained introspection to analyse the elements of conscious experience, the program his student Edward Titchener later named structuralism and took to America. Less well known but equally important is that Wundt also developed the concept of Volkerpsychologie, usually translated as folk psychology or cultural psychology. In his later years Wundt devoted a monumental ten-volume work to this subject, arguing that the higher mental processes, those shaped by social and cultural life such as language, myth, religion, customs, and art, could not be studied through laboratory experiment and introspection alone, because they arise from collective human activity over history. Instead, these phenomena had to be understood by examining the products of communities and cultures. Thus Wundt distinguished between an experimental, physiological psychology of individual immediate experience and a folk psychology of the collective mind expressed through culture. This dual programme makes Wundt a foundational figure for both experimental and social-cultural psychology. Because the concept of Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) was Wundt's, he is the correct answer.", "topic": "Wundt Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology)", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "What type of psychological test is the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)?", "options": {"a": "Aptitude test", "b": "Intelligence test", "c": "Attitude test", "d": "Achievement test"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) is the most widely used individually administered intelligence test for adults. It was developed by David Wechsler, who defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally, and deal effectively with the environment. Unlike the earlier Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916), which yielded a single ratio IQ (William Stern, 1912), Wechsler's scales produce a Full Scale IQ along with index scores for distinct domains such as verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning (split into visual spatial and fluid reasoning in the WAIS-5, 2024), working memory, and processing speed, using a deviation IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. The WAIS is therefore squarely an intelligence test, measuring general cognitive ability. The distractors name other categories: an aptitude test predicts capacity for a specific future task or training, an attitude test measures evaluative opinions, and an achievement test measures knowledge already acquired in academic subjects. Because the WAIS is a standardized measure of general intelligence, option b is correct.", "topic": "WAIS as intelligence test", "fig": "David Wechsler"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a stage of group formation?", "options": {"a": "Storming", "b": "Proximity", "c": "Norming", "d": "Performing"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "This question refers to Bruce Tuckman's classic model of group development, published in 1965, which describes the predictable sequence teams pass through as they form and mature. The stages are, in order: Forming, in which members come together, are polite and tentative, and orient themselves to the task; Storming, in which conflict emerges as individuals assert their views, compete for roles, and test boundaries; Norming, in which the group resolves those tensions, establishes shared rules and expectations, and develops cohesion and trust; and Performing, in which the group functions smoothly and channels its energy into achieving its goals. Tuckman later added a fifth stage, Adjourning, for the disbanding of a group once its work is complete. The rhyming labels (forming, storming, norming, performing) make the sequence easy to remember and are exactly what an exam expects you to recognise. 'Proximity' is not part of this model at all; it is a concept from the psychology of attraction and Gestalt perception, referring to nearness as a factor in forming relationships or grouping stimuli. Because proximity does not belong among Tuckman's stages, it is the item that is 'not a stage of group formation', which makes it the correct answer to this negatively worded question.", "topic": "Tuckman stages of group development", "fig": "Bruce Tuckman"}, {"q": "'Displacement' is a defense mechanism typically seen in:", "options": {"a": "Hysteria", "b": "Phobias", "c": "Obsessive-compulsive disorder", "d": "Depression"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "Displacement is a defence mechanism typically seen in phobias, so the answer is b. Displacement is the ego defence in which an emotion, impulse, or drive, often anxiety or hostility, is redirected from its original, threatening object onto a substitute object that is safer or more acceptable. In the psychoanalytic theory of phobias, the classic formulation, illustrated by Freud's case of Little Hans, holds that the child's unacceptable anxiety, originally attached to an internal conflict or a forbidden impulse toward a parent, is displaced onto an external, symbolic, and avoidable object, in Hans's case a horse. The phobic object then represents and stands in for the true, intolerable source of anxiety; by fearing and avoiding the external object, the person keeps the underlying conflict out of awareness and confines the anxiety to a circumscribed, manageable target. So phobia is understood psychodynamically as anxiety displaced onto a symbolic external stimulus. This is why displacement is the defence characteristically associated with phobic disorders. The exam discriminator is the specific psychodynamic mapping of displacement onto phobias (anxiety shifted onto an external symbolic object), distinguishing it from the defences classically tied to other conditions: conversion and repression in hysteria, the isolation undoing reaction formation triad in OCD, and introjection or turning against the self in depression.", "topic": "Displacement defence in phobias"}, {"q": "Which of the four lobes of the brain is responsible for interpreting sensory information?", "options": {"a": "Frontal lobe", "b": "Occipital lobe", "c": "Parietal lobe", "d": "Temporal lobe"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The correct answer is the parietal lobe. The parietal lobe, located at the top and toward the back of each cerebral hemisphere, is the brain's principal centre for integrating and interpreting somatosensory information. It contains the primary somatosensory cortex (the postcentral gyrus), which receives and processes sensations of touch, pressure, temperature, pain, and proprioception (the sense of body position) from the entire body, organised as the sensory homunculus (Wilder Penfield). Beyond raw sensation, the parietal association cortex integrates input from multiple senses to build a coherent representation of the body and of space, supporting functions such as spatial awareness, navigation, hand-eye coordination, and attention to the location of objects. Damage to the parietal lobe can produce striking deficits, including hemispatial neglect (ignoring one side of space, typically after right parietal damage), agraphesthesia (inability to recognise numbers traced on the skin), and difficulties with spatial orientation. The question asks which lobe interprets sensory information, and because the parietal lobe houses the somatosensory cortex and integrates sensory input into perception of the body and surrounding space, it is the correct choice. Each of the four lobes has a characteristic functional emphasis, and the parietal lobe's emphasis is sensory integration.", "topic": "Parietal lobe sensory processing"}, {"q": "According to Jean Piaget's theory of play development, what is the first stage of child play that emerges during infancy?", "options": {"a": "Symbolic play", "b": "Functional play", "c": "Games with rules", "d": "Constructive play"}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The answer is functional play because in Jean Piaget's account of how play develops alongside cognition, functional play (also called practice play or sensorimotor play) is the earliest form, appearing in infancy during the sensorimotor stage. Piaget, the Swiss psychologist whose theory of cognitive development frames children as active constructors of knowledge, saw play as reflecting the child's current cognitive level. Functional play consists of simple, repetitive physical actions performed for the sheer pleasure and mastery of doing them, such as an infant repeatedly shaking a rattle, banging objects together, splashing water, or putting things in and out of a container. These actions map directly onto the sensorimotor infant's way of understanding the world through movement and sensation, hence the name. As the child matures cognitively, play develops through further stages: symbolic or pretend play emerges in the preoperational period (roughly ages 2 to 7), when the child can let one thing represent another, using a banana as a telephone or feeding a doll; constructive play involves purposefully building and creating; and games with rules appear in middle childhood once the child can grasp and follow shared, cooperative rules, reflecting more advanced social and logical thinking. Because the question asks for the first stage emerging in infancy, and functional practice play corresponds to the sensorimotor infant, that is the correct answer.", "topic": "Piaget practice/functional play first", "fig": "Jean Piaget"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes \"behavioural activation\" in psychotherapy?", "options": {"a": "Encouraging clients to engage in meaningful activities to counteract depression", "b": "Analysing early childhood experiences", "c": "Relaxation training for anxiety", "d": "Hypnosis for symptom relief"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "Behavioural activation is a structured, present-focused treatment for depression that grew out of the behavioural tradition. Its logic traces to Charles Ferster and later Peter Lewinsohn, who argued that depression is maintained by a loss of response-contingent positive reinforcement: when someone feels low they withdraw, do less, and contact fewer sources of reward, which deepens the low mood in a vicious cycle. Neil Jacobson and colleagues then isolated activation as a powerful stand-alone treatment, showing in a component analysis that it worked as well as full cognitive therapy for many patients. The core method is to help the client gradually re-engage in meaningful, valued and pleasurable activities through activity monitoring, scheduling and graded task assignment, acting from the outside in rather than waiting to feel motivated first. By increasing contact with natural reinforcers, mood lifts and avoidance shrinks. The distractors describe other approaches: exploring early childhood is psychodynamic, relaxation training is a behavioural technique aimed at anxiety, and hypnosis is a separate modality entirely. The defining feature of behavioural activation is deliberately scheduling rewarding action to break the withdrawal-depression loop. A helpful anchor: it treats depression by changing what you do before changing how you feel, so the phrase 'engage in meaningful activities to counteract depression' captures its essence precisely.", "topic": "Behavioural activation for depression"}, {"q": "According to DSM-5, which of the following is required for a diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?", "options": {"a": "Delayed speech alone", "b": "Persistent deficits in social communication and interaction across multiple contexts", "c": "Intellectual disability only", "d": "Hyperactivity and impulsivity"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "Autism was first described by Leo Kanner (1943), and a verbally fluent picture by Hans Asperger (1944). DSM-IV used a triad (social interaction, communication, restricted behaviour) and separate diagnoses such as autistic disorder and Asperger's disorder. DSM-5 (2013) merged these into one autism spectrum disorder with two criterion domains. Criterion A: persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts, shown in all three of social-emotional reciprocity (for example, failure of back-and-forth conversation), nonverbal communicative behaviours (poor eye contact, few gestures), and developing, maintaining and understanding relationships. Criterion B: restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, at least two of four: stereotyped movements, speech or object use (echolalia, lining up toys); insistence on sameness; highly restricted, fixated interests; and hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input. Symptoms must be present in the early developmental period (C), cause clinically significant impairment (D), and not be better explained by intellectual disability or global developmental delay (E). Language and intellectual impairment are now specifiers ('with or without accompanying language impairment', 'with or without accompanying intellectual impairment'), and severity is rated Level 1 to 3 by the support needed. Example: Kabir, aged four, speaks in full sentences but repeats film dialogue, avoids peers and screams when his route to school changes; he meets criteria despite normal speech. Neighbour: social (pragmatic) communication disorder has Criterion A type deficits without Criterion B. Option b is right because persistent social communication and interaction deficits across contexts form Criterion A, which every ASD diagnosis requires, together with Criterion B.", "topic": "DSM-5 autism core criteria"}, {"q": "Uniting various qualitative methods with quantitative methods can be called as:", "options": {"a": "Coalition", "b": "Triangulation", "c": "Multidimensional approach", "d": "Co-operative inquiry"}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "Combining qualitative and quantitative methods is called triangulation, so the answer is b. Triangulation is the strategy of using more than one method, data source, theory, or investigator to study the same phenomenon, so that the strengths of one approach compensate for the weaknesses of another and convergent findings increase confidence in the conclusions. The term borrows from navigation and surveying, where a position is fixed by taking bearings from multiple points. Norman Denzin distinguished several types: methodological triangulation (mixing methods such as qualitative interviews with quantitative surveys, which is exactly what the question describes), data triangulation (different times, places, or samples), investigator triangulation (multiple researchers), and theory triangulation (multiple theoretical lenses). The rationale is that quantitative methods offer breadth, measurement, and generalisability while qualitative methods offer depth, meaning, and context, so uniting them yields a richer and more trustworthy picture and guards against the bias inherent in any single method. This is the conceptual core of mixed methods research. The discriminating cue in the item is uniting various qualitative methods with quantitative methods, which is the textbook description of methodological triangulation. The exam point is to attach the navigation derived word triangulation to the idea of cross checking findings through multiple complementary approaches.", "topic": "Triangulation of qualitative and quantitative methods"}, {"q": "The researcher who formulated the theory which explains how we form artificial concepts rather than natural concepts by developing a hypothesis is:", "options": {"a": "Clark Hull", "b": "Eleanor Rosch", "c": "Jerome Bruner", "d": "John Watson"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The researcher who explained how we form artificial rather than natural concepts by developing and testing hypotheses is Jerome Bruner, so the answer is c. Concepts are mental categories that group objects, events, or ideas by shared features. Cognitive psychology distinguishes natural concepts, which are fuzzy, real world categories like bird or furniture that we learn through everyday experience and that are organised around prototypes (a line associated with Eleanor Rosch), from artificial concepts, which are precisely defined categories created for study, learned by figuring out the rule that defines membership. Jerome Bruner, in his classic work A Study of Thinking (with Goodnow and Austin), investigated how people learn artificial concepts in the laboratory and described concept attainment as an active process of hypothesis testing. The learner forms a hypothesis about which features define the concept, tests it against instances classified as examples or non examples, and revises the hypothesis based on feedback, using systematic strategies such as conservative focusing. This portrayed the concept learner as an active, strategic problem solver developing and checking hypotheses, in contrast to passive association. The exam discriminator is the pairing of artificial concept formation through hypothesis development and testing with Bruner, distinguishing his work from Rosch's prototype based account of natural concepts and from Hull and Watson's behaviourist frameworks.", "topic": "Bruner hypothesis testing artificial concepts", "fig": "Jerome Bruner"}, {"q": "Which of the following psychologists is associated with the development of the first intelligence test?", "options": {"a": "Freud", "b": "Piaget", "c": "Binet", "d": "Skinner"}, "correct": "c", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The first practical intelligence test was developed by the French psychologist Alfred Binet, together with his collaborator Theodore Simon. In 1904 the French government commissioned Binet to devise a way to identify schoolchildren who needed special educational assistance, and the resulting Binet-Simon Scale (1905) measured children's performance on tasks of judgment, comprehension, and reasoning. Its 1908 revision grouped the tasks by age and introduced the mental level (later called mental age), the age level at which a child was performing. This scale was the foundation of modern intelligence testing. It was later revised by Lewis Terman at Stanford University into the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, which adopted the intelligence quotient (IQ), a term from William Stern, computed as mental age divided by chronological age times 100. The distractors are unrelated to this achievement: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, Jean Piaget studied stages of cognitive development (though he briefly worked in Binet's laboratory), and B. F. Skinner pioneered operant conditioning. Because Alfred Binet developed the first intelligence test, option c is correct.", "topic": "Binet first intelligence test", "fig": "Alfred Binet"}, {"q": "Who reformulated the \"frustration-aggression hypothesis\"?", "options": {"a": "Dollard", "b": "Miller", "c": "Berkowitz", "d": "Lazarus"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The correct answer is Berkowitz. The original frustration-aggression hypothesis was put forward in 1939 by a Yale group, John Dollard, Neal Miller, Leonard Doob, Orval Mowrer, and Robert Sears, who proposed a strong and direct link: frustration (blocking of goal-directed behavior) always leads to some form of aggression, and aggression is always the result of frustration. This absolute formulation proved too rigid, since frustration does not always produce aggression and aggression can arise without frustration. Leonard Berkowitz reformulated the hypothesis in a more nuanced, cognitive-associationist way. He argued that frustration produces a readiness for aggression by generating negative affect (anger), but whether aggression actually occurs depends on the presence of situational cues, particularly aggressive cues in the environment. His famous cue-arousal or weapons-effect research showed that the mere presence of a weapon could increase aggressive responses. Berkowitz thus shifted the model from an inevitable frustration-to-aggression chain to one in which negative emotion plus aggression-eliciting cues jointly determine aggressive behavior, integrating cognition and environmental context. Because the significant reformulation of the frustration-aggression hypothesis is credited to Leonard Berkowitz, option c is correct.", "topic": "Berkowitz reformulated frustration-aggression hypothesis", "fig": "Leonard Berkowitz"}, {"q": "The concept of the “collective unconscious” was given by:", "options": {"a": "Sigmund Freud", "b": "Alfred Adler", "c": "Carl Jung", "d": "Karen Horney"}, "correct": "c", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is Carl Jung. The collective unconscious is the cornerstone of Jung's analytical psychology and the feature that most sharply distinguishes him from Freud. Whereas Freud's unconscious was personal, built from an individual's own repressed experiences, Jung proposed a deeper layer of the psyche shared by all human beings, inherited rather than acquired. This collective unconscious is populated by archetypes: universal, innate templates or predispositions that shape how we experience the world, such as the Mother, the Hero, the Shadow (the disowned dark side of the self), the Anima and Animus (the feminine within men and masculine within women), and the Self (the archetype of wholeness and integration). Jung drew evidence from the recurrence of similar myths, symbols, and motifs across unrelated cultures and from the universal imagery appearing in dreams. The collective unconscious does not contain specific memories but rather inherited potentials for organising experience. This concept underpins Jungian therapy, where the goal of individuation is to integrate conscious and unconscious material, including archetypal contents, into a balanced whole. Understanding it is essential for distinguishing the three major depth-psychology systems of Freud, Adler, and Jung.", "topic": "Jung collective unconscious", "fig": "Carl Jung"}, {"q": "Adreno-cortico-tropic hormone is released by", "options": {"a": "Adrenal gland.", "b": "Anterior pituitary gland.", "c": "Posterior pituitary gland.", "d": "Sweat gland."}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The stress response runs along the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, and knowing which gland does what is essential. The hypothalamus first releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH). CRH travels a short portal route to the anterior pituitary (the adenohypophysis), which responds by secreting adrenocorticotropic hormone, ACTH. ACTH then enters the bloodstream and acts on the adrenal cortex, stimulating it to produce glucocorticoids such as cortisol. Cortisol mobilizes energy and, through negative feedback, dampens further CRH and ACTH release. So the anterior pituitary is the source of ACTH; the adrenal gland is merely its downstream target. It helps to separate the two lobes of the pituitary: the anterior lobe is true glandular tissue that manufactures its own hormones (ACTH, growth hormone, TSH, FSH, LH, prolactin), whereas the posterior lobe is neural tissue that only stores and releases hormones made in the hypothalamus (oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone). Mixing up 'the gland that releases the hormone' with 'the gland the hormone acts on' is the trap here, and the HPA cascade makes the direction unmistakable: hypothalamus to anterior pituitary to adrenal cortex. Therefore ACTH is released by the anterior pituitary gland.", "topic": "ACTH from anterior pituitary"}, {"q": "This theorist argued that newborn creatures imprint on their caregivers during a critical period in early development.", "options": {"a": "Mary Ainsworth", "b": "John Bowlby", "c": "Erik Erikson", "d": "Konrad Lorenz"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The theorist is Konrad Lorenz, the Austrian ethologist who demonstrated and named imprinting. Imprinting is a rapid, largely irreversible form of early learning in which a young animal forms an attachment to and follows the first moving object it encounters, normally its mother, during a fixed and brief critical period shortly after birth or hatching. Lorenz famously showed this with greylag goslings: hatchlings exposed to him rather than to their mother during the sensitive window imprinted on him and followed him as if he were their parent, even preferring him to their own species. Imprinting illustrates several ethological principles: behaviour can be biologically prepared and triggered by specific stimuli, learning can be time locked to a critical period, and the function is adaptive because following the mother keeps the vulnerable young protected and fed. Lorenz's work, alongside Niko Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch, earned a Nobel Prize and laid groundwork that John Bowlby drew on for attachment theory in humans. The exam discriminator is the combination of newborn creatures, imprint, caregivers, and critical period, all of which are Lorenz's signature terms. Bowlby and Ainsworth applied attachment ideas to human infants, but the specific phenomenon of imprinting during a critical period in young animals is Lorenz's contribution.", "topic": "Lorenz imprinting critical period", "fig": "Konrad Lorenz"}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "options": {"a": "Flooding", "b": "Token economies", "c": "Systematic desensitisation", "d": "Aversion therapies"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is token economies (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968). It is important to distinguish therapies derived from classical (Pavlovian) conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov, from those derived from operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner. Classical conditioning concerns associations between stimuli and reflexive responses, and several exposure-based and aversive therapies flow from it. Flooding exposes the client, all at once and at full intensity, to the feared stimulus without escape, so that the conditioned fear response extinguishes when no harm follows. Systematic desensitization, developed by Joseph Wolpe, pairs graded exposure to the feared stimulus with relaxation (counterconditioning via reciprocal inhibition). Aversion therapy pairs an undesirable behaviour or stimulus (such as alcohol) with an unpleasant unconditioned stimulus (such as a nausea-inducing drug) so that the behaviour comes to elicit aversion. All three rest on stimulus-response associations, that is, classical conditioning. Token economies, by contrast, are based on operant conditioning: desired behaviours are reinforced with tokens (secondary reinforcers) that can later be exchanged for backup rewards, shaping voluntary behaviour through its consequences. Because token economies work through operant reinforcement rather than classical stimulus-stimulus association, they are the technique not developed from classical conditioning principles, making option b correct.", "topic": "Classical vs operant derived therapies", "fig": "Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin"}, {"q": "Substance use disorder has the following patterns EXCEPT:", "options": {"a": "Acute intoxication and withdrawal", "b": "Dependence Syndrome", "c": "Harmful use", "d": "Drug abuse"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The correct answer is drug abuse, because it is the one term in the list that is not a recognized diagnostic pattern within the ICD framework for substance use disorders; the others (acute intoxication and withdrawal, dependence syndrome, and harmful use) are formal ICD-10 categories. The question reflects ICD-10's classification of mental and behavioral disorders due to psychoactive substance use, which organizes substance-related conditions into specific clinical syndromes. Acute intoxication is the transient disturbance of consciousness, cognition, perception, affect, or behavior directly following substance use; withdrawal state is the cluster of symptoms occurring on reducing or stopping a substance after repeated heavy use. Dependence syndrome is the core construct, defined by a cluster of features including a strong desire or compulsion to take the substance, impaired control over use, a withdrawal state, tolerance, neglect of alternative interests, and persistence despite harm. Harmful use is the ICD-10 pattern of substance use that is actually causing damage to physical or mental health, without yet meeting full dependence criteria. The term drug abuse, by contrast, belongs to the older DSM tradition (DSM-IV used substance abuse versus dependence) and is not an ICD diagnostic category, which is why it is the exception. The concept to master is the ICD-10 substance-use scheme and how it differs from DSM terminology, since Indian clinical training and RCI-regulated exams predominantly follow ICD.", "topic": "ICD-10 substance use categories"}, {"q": "Which of the following best reflects a limitation of correlational research designs?", "options": {"a": "They can establish causality between variables.", "b": "They cannot determine the direction of influence between variables.", "c": "They always require a random assignment of subjects.", "d": "They are seldom used in psychological research."}, "correct": "b", "section": "research", "explanation": "Correlational research measures the degree of relationship between two or more variables as they naturally occur, without manipulating them. Its central limitation is the maxim that correlation does not imply causation. Two problems drive this. First is the directionality problem: if variables A and B are related, the design cannot tell whether A causes B, B causes A, or the influence is mutual. Second is the third-variable problem: an unmeasured confound may cause both A and B, producing a spurious association. For instance, a correlation between ice-cream sales and drowning is driven by a third variable, hot weather, not by either causing the other. Only a true experiment, with manipulation of an independent variable and random assignment to conditions, can support causal claims. Option a is false because correlational designs cannot establish causality; option c is false because random assignment is a feature of experiments; option d is false because correlational methods are ubiquitous. Option b correctly names the inability to determine the direction of influence, which is a genuine and defining limitation.", "topic": "Correlation cannot show causal direction"}, {"q": "Who introduced the concept of \"flow\" in psychology?", "options": {"a": "Carl Rogers", "b": "Abraham Maslow", "c": "Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi", "d": "Viktor Frankl"}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The correct answer is Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi. Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi introduced the concept of flow, the mental state of complete absorption in an activity in which a person is fully immersed, energised, and focused, losing track of time and self-consciousness. Flow occurs when the challenge of a task is well matched to the person's skill level: too easy and one is bored, too hard and one is anxious, but in the balanced sweet spot, engagement becomes optimal and intrinsically rewarding. Csikszentmihalyi is a founding figure of positive psychology, and his work on flow is central to understanding intrinsic motivation, creativity, and well-being, so option C is correct. Carl Rogers (option A), a humanistic psychologist, developed client-centred (person-centred) therapy and the idea of unconditional positive regard and the fully functioning person, not flow. Abraham Maslow (option B) is known for the hierarchy of needs and for the concept of peak experiences and self-actualisation; peak experiences resemble flow in being deeply absorbing, but they are a distinct idea, and Maslow did not coin flow. Viktor Frankl (option D), a survivor of the Holocaust, founded logotherapy and emphasised the human search for meaning as the primary motivational force. The concept of flow remains a cornerstone of research on optimal experience.", "topic": "Csikszentmihalyi concept of flow", "fig": "Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi"}, {"q": "Identify the correct option for Projective tests of Personality: i) Rorschach & TAT, ii) Eysenck Personality Questionnaire, iii) 16 PF, iv) MCMI-III.", "options": {"a": "Both i and ii", "b": "Only i", "c": "i, ii and iv", "d": "None"}, "correct": "b", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "Only Rorschach and TAT are projective tests, so 'Only i' is correct. The crucial distinction is between projective and objective (self-report) personality tests. Projective tests present deliberately ambiguous, unstructured stimuli and ask the person to interpret them, on the assumption (the projective hypothesis) that people project their unconscious needs, conflicts, and personality onto the vague material because there is no obviously right answer to constrain them. The Rorschach Inkblot Test (ten symmetrical inkblots, devised by Hermann Rorschach in 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT, ambiguous scene cards about which the person tells a story, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan) are the two classic projective instruments. By contrast, objective tests use structured items with fixed response formats (true/false or rating scales) that are scored against norms. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (measuring extraversion, neuroticism, and psychoticism), Cattell's 16 Personality Factor questionnaire (the 16 PF), and the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III) are all objective self-report inventories, not projective. So among the four listed, only item (i) names projective tests, making 'Only i' the right answer.", "topic": "Identifying projective versus objective tests"}, {"q": "Which social psychological theory suggests that individuals perceive others in terms of their positive or negative traits, leading to a \"halo\" effect?", "options": {"a": "Implicit personality theory", "b": "Self-perception theory", "c": "Trait-ascription bias", "d": "Primacy effect"}, "correct": "a", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is a: implicit personality theory is the set of unspoken beliefs each of us holds about which traits go together, and it is what produces the halo effect. Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri (1954) gave the idea its name. When we meet someone we know only a few of their traits, so we fill in the rest from our private 'theory' of personality: warm people must also be generous, clever people must also be hard-working, attractive people must also be kind. The halo effect is the best-known product of this filling-in. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after finding that army officers who rated a soldier high on physique also rated him high on intelligence, loyalty and leadership; one good impression spread to unrelated traits. It also works in reverse, when one bad trait darkens everything (the 'horns' effect). For example, Kavya's new colleague Rohan is cheerful and well dressed, so she assumes he is also competent and honest, though she has no evidence on either. Halo effects bias interviews, teachers' grading and performance appraisals, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. Option a is right because only implicit personality theory explains why a single positive or negative trait colours our judgements of all the others. Exam trap: the primacy effect (Solomon Asch, 1946) also shapes first impressions, but it is about the order of information (early traits weigh most), not about one trait spreading to the rest.", "topic": "Implicit personality theory and halo", "fig": "Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri"}, {"q": "The term 'Inferiority Complex' was coined by:", "options": {"a": "Franz Alexander", "b": "Alfred Adler", "c": "Gordon Allport", "d": "Carl Jung"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The answer is Alfred Adler because the term inferiority complex was coined by Adler, the founder of Individual Psychology and an early associate of Freud who broke away to develop his own theory emphasizing social and motivational factors over sexual drives. Adler proposed that all human beings begin life with normal feelings of inferiority, because children are small, dependent, and surrounded by more capable adults. These feelings act as a healthy motivating force, driving people to strive for competence, mastery, and what he called superiority, meaning growth and self-improvement rather than dominance over others. When this normal striving goes wrong, two problems can arise. An inferiority complex is an exaggerated, persistent sense of inadequacy and helplessness in which the person feels overwhelmed and unable to compensate, often stemming from organ inferiority, pampering, or neglect in childhood. Its counterpart, the superiority complex, is an overcompensating facade of boastfulness or arrogance that masks the underlying inferiority. Central to Adler's system are also concepts like striving for superiority, the style of life, social interest, and birth order. Recognizing inferiority complex as distinctly Adlerian helps separate him from Freud, who emphasized the unconscious and psychosexual stages, and from Jung, who developed the collective unconscious and archetypes. So the coining of inferiority complex belongs to Alfred Adler.", "topic": "Adler inferiority complex", "fig": "Alfred Adler"}, {"q": "Which of the following is an inhibitory neurotransmitter?", "options": {"a": "Norepinephrine", "b": "GABA", "c": "Dopamine", "d": "Epinephrine"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "Neurotransmitters are broadly grouped by their effect on the receiving neuron's membrane. An excitatory transmitter depolarizes the postsynaptic cell, making it more likely to fire; an inhibitory transmitter hyperpolarizes it, making firing less likely. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter of the mammalian central nervous system. It works mainly through GABA-A receptors, which are chloride ion channels: when GABA binds, chloride flows into the neuron, the inside becomes more negative, and the cell is pushed away from its firing threshold. This calming, dampening action is why GABA underlies the sedative and anxiolytic effects of drugs that enhance it, such as benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and alcohol, which potentiate GABA-A signalling. Its counterpart, glutamate, is the main excitatory transmitter, and the balance between the two keeps neural activity stable; too little GABAergic inhibition contributes to anxiety and seizures. The other options are catecholamines (norepinephrine, dopamine, epinephrine) that are predominantly excitatory or modulatory and drive arousal, reward, and stress. For exam purposes, remember the pairing: GABA equals inhibition, glutamate equals excitation. That makes GABA the correct choice for an inhibitory neurotransmitter.", "topic": "GABA inhibitory neurotransmitter"}, {"q": "A tribe of Native Americans have a specific model of parenting that has been practised for generations. Mothers respond quickly to infant distress, which leads to decreased episodes of crying and tantrums. They also believe in the power of touch, and hold their children almost constantly through the first three years of the baby's life, which also leads to decreased distress. Generations of the tribe's children are quiet, even tempered, obedient, and respectful. The behaviour of the tribe's children is best described by which of the following terms?", "options": {"a": "Covert Behaviour", "b": "Innate Behaviour", "c": "Complex Behaviour", "d": "Learnt Behaviour"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The correct answer is learnt behaviour. Learnt (learned) behaviour is behaviour acquired through experience and environmental influence rather than inherited genetically. In this scenario, the calm, even-tempered, obedient behaviour of the tribe's children arises directly from consistent parenting practices: prompt responsiveness to distress and near-constant physical contact during the crucial first three years. Because the behaviour is shaped by nurture, socialisation, and repeated experience rather than being present at birth, it is learned rather than innate. This connects to attachment theory by John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, who argued that sensitive, responsive caregiving fosters secure attachment and emotional regulation, and to social learning theory by Albert Bandura, who showed that children acquire behaviour patterns through modelling and reinforcement in their environment. The key contrast is nature versus nurture: innate behaviour (option B) is genetically pre-programmed and universal, whereas the described temperament is transmitted culturally across generations through childrearing methods. Covert behaviour concerns hidden internal states, and complex behaviour is a vague descriptor that ignores the causal role of environment. The item tests whether the student recognises environmentally acquired behaviour as learned.", "topic": "Learnt behaviour through parenting practices"}, {"q": "Systematic desensitisation, in which a client resolves irrational fears through gradual exposure to the fear-producing stimulus, was first developed as a therapy technique by:", "options": {"a": "Carl Rogers", "b": "Joseph Wolpe", "c": "Sigmund Freud", "d": "Albert Bandura"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "Systematic desensitisation was developed by Joseph Wolpe in the 1950s, making b correct. Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist working within the behaviour-therapy tradition, built the technique on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: the idea that two incompatible physiological states cannot be experienced at the same time, so if a person is deeply relaxed they cannot simultaneously feel intense anxiety. By repeatedly pairing relaxation with the feared stimulus, the relaxation response comes to inhibit and eventually replace the anxiety response, a form of counterconditioning. The procedure has three core components. First, the therapist trains the client in deep muscle relaxation, often using Jacobson's progressive relaxation. Second, the client and therapist construct an anxiety hierarchy, ranking fear-provoking situations from least to most threatening on a scale of subjective units of distress. Third, while maintaining relaxation, the client is exposed, usually first in imagination and gradually in reality, to each step of the hierarchy, moving up only when the lower item no longer evokes anxiety. Because exposure is graded and paired with relaxation, the client never becomes overwhelmed, distinguishing it from flooding, where the person confronts the most feared stimulus immediately and at full intensity. Systematic desensitisation remains a foundational evidence-based treatment for specific phobias and other anxiety conditions and is one of the clearest applications of classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) principles to therapy.", "topic": "Wolpe systematic desensitisation", "fig": "Joseph Wolpe"}, {"q": "Identify the 'odd one out' from the groups:", "options": {"a": "Paranoid personality disorder", "b": "Schizoid personality disorder", "c": "Schizotypal personality disorder", "d": "Borderline personality disorder"}, "correct": "d", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The correct answer is Borderline personality disorder, which is the odd one out because the other three (paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal) all belong to DSM Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, whereas borderline personality disorder belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional, and erratic cluster. The DSM organizes the ten personality disorders into three clusters based on descriptive similarity. Cluster A includes paranoid (pervasive distrust and suspiciousness), schizoid (detachment from social relationships and restricted emotional expression), and schizotypal (acute discomfort in relationships plus cognitive-perceptual distortions and eccentric behavior); these share a flavor of social oddness and are thought to share some genetic relationship with the schizophrenia spectrum. Cluster B includes antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic disorders, marked by emotional dysregulation, impulsivity, and interpersonal turmoil. Cluster C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive) is the anxious, fearful cluster. Borderline personality disorder specifically is defined by pervasive instability of affect, self-image, and relationships together with marked impulsivity, including fear of abandonment, identity disturbance, self-harm, and chronic emptiness. The concept to master is the cluster structure itself: grouping the disorders by their core thematic resemblance is exactly what lets you spot that three options cluster together as eccentric (Cluster A) while borderline stands apart as dramatic-emotional (Cluster B).", "topic": "Cluster A personality disorders"}, {"q": "Those sampling methods which clearly specify the likelihood of inclusion of each element in the sample are known as:", "options": {"a": "Probability sampling methods", "b": "Non-Probability sampling methods", "c": "Statistical sampling", "d": "Empirical sampling"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "These are probability sampling methods, so the answer is a. The defining feature of probability sampling is that every element in the population has a known, non zero chance of being selected, and that chance can be specified. Because the selection is governed by chance rather than by the researcher's judgment or convenience, probability sampling supports unbiased estimation, the calculation of sampling error, and statistical generalisation from sample to population. The main probability designs are simple random sampling, where each unit has an equal chance; systematic sampling, selecting every kth unit from a random start; stratified sampling, dividing the population into strata and sampling within each; and cluster sampling, randomly selecting whole groups. The contrast is non probability sampling, including convenience, quota, purposive, and snowball methods, in which the probability of inclusion is unknown and selection rests on accessibility or judgment, so results cannot be generalised with known precision. The exam discriminator is the exact phrase clearly specify the likelihood of inclusion of each element, which is the textbook definition of probability sampling. The deeper point is that knowing the inclusion probabilities is what licenses inferential statistics and estimates of confidence, which is why probability sampling is preferred whenever representative, generalisable conclusions are the goal.", "topic": "Probability sampling definition"}, {"q": "Among monocular cues for depth perception is", "options": {"a": "Convergence.", "b": "Retinal disparity.", "c": "Assimilation.", "d": "Interposition."}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "Depth cues split into two families. Binocular cues need both eyes working together, while monocular cues can be picked up with a single eye and are what allow a flat painting or photograph to convey depth. Interposition (also called occlusion) is a monocular cue: when one object partly blocks another, the covered object is judged to be farther away. Other monocular cues studied by perception researchers, and organised into the pictorial cues emphasised by the Gestalt tradition and later by James Gibson, include relative size, linear perspective, texture gradient, aerial perspective, and motion parallax. The two great binocular cues are convergence, the muscular signal from the eyes rotating inward for near objects, and retinal (binocular) disparity, the small difference between the two eyes' images that Charles Wheatstone demonstrated with his stereoscope and that the brain fuses into a vivid sense of solidity. The trap in this item is that convergence and retinal disparity are both real, powerful depth cues, but both are binocular, so neither answers a question asking specifically for a monocular cue. Assimilation is a distractor borrowed from Jean Piaget's developmental theory and has nothing to do with perceiving depth. Interposition is the only listed cue that a one-eyed observer could still exploit.", "topic": "Monocular depth cue interposition"}, {"q": "A response style or bias, in which there is tendency to agree with test items regardless of their contents is called:", "options": {"a": "Halo effect", "b": "Central tendency effect", "c": "Social desirability effect", "d": "Acquiescence effect"}, "correct": "d", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is the acquiescence effect. Acquiescence (also called the acquiescence response set or yea-saying) is a response bias in which a person tends to agree with, or answer yes or true to, test items regardless of their content, rather than responding to what each item actually asks. Such respondents go along with statements out of habit, agreeableness, deference, or carelessness, which distorts their scores because their answers reflect a general tendency to agree rather than their true position. This threatens the validity of questionnaires and personality inventories. The classic safeguard against acquiescence is to balance the scale with both positively and negatively worded items (reverse-keyed items), so that consistent agreement cannot artificially inflate a score in one direction and genuine respondents must read each item. Acquiescence is one of several response styles or biases that distort self-report measures. Others include social desirability (answering in ways that present oneself favourably rather than truthfully), the central tendency bias (avoiding extreme options and clustering responses in the middle of a rating scale), and the halo effect (letting a global impression colour specific ratings). Because the defining feature here is agreeing with items irrespective of their content, the acquiescence effect is the precise answer.", "topic": "Acquiescence response set"}, {"q": "The physical attractiveness of a person, or any other trait, can influence multiple judgements and lead to a more favourable view of the whole personality. This tendency is called:", "options": {"a": "Primacy effect", "b": "Pygmalion effect", "c": "Halo effect", "d": "Non-common effect"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: when one attractive trait colours our judgement of the whole person, it is called the halo effect. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after studying how army officers rated their soldiers on physique, intelligence, leadership and character. The ratings correlated far too highly: a soldier judged handsome and well built was also rated more intelligent and more loyal, as if one good impression cast a glow over everything else. The halo is a general impression, or one striking trait, spreading to other, unrelated traits. Physical attractiveness is the strongest trigger. Karen Dion, Ellen Berscheid and Elaine Walster (1972) showed students photographs of attractive, average and unattractive people; the attractive ones were judged kinder, more sociable, more successful and happier: 'what is beautiful is good'. The reverse, one bad trait dragging down the whole judgement, is sometimes called the horns effect. The halo also biases ratings by teachers, interviewers and clinicians, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. At a job interview, for example, Rohan is smart, confident and well dressed, and the panel also assumes he is honest and hard-working, although they have no evidence on those traits. Option c is right because the stem describes one trait shaping many unrelated judgements and a more favourable view of the whole personality. Exam trap: the Pygmalion effect also involves expectations about a person, but it concerns how those expectations change the person's actual performance; the halo effect is a bias in the perceiver's judgement only.", "topic": "Halo effect", "fig": "Edward Thorndike"}, {"q": "Which of the following defense mechanism is seen in patients with borderline personality disorder?", "options": {"a": "Splitting", "b": "Regression", "c": "Undoing", "d": "Sublimation"}, "correct": "a", "section": "personality", "explanation": "The correct answer is splitting. Splitting is the central, characteristic defence mechanism of borderline personality disorder (BPD). Splitting is the inability to integrate the positive and negative qualities of oneself or others into a coherent, balanced whole, so that people (including the self) are experienced as either all good or all bad, with no middle ground. A person using splitting may idealise someone one moment (seeing them as perfect, wonderful, the only one who understands) and then, after a minor disappointment, abruptly devalue the same person (seeing them as cruel, worthless, an enemy). This produces the intense, unstable, and stormy interpersonal relationships that are a core feature of BPD, along with an unstable sense of self, emotional dysregulation, fear of abandonment, and impulsivity. Splitting is rooted in Melanie Klein's object-relations theory and Otto Kernberg's account of borderline personality organisation, where the failure to integrate good and bad object-representations is seen as the underlying structural problem. The all-good versus all-bad oscillation directly explains the dramatic swings in how borderline patients view their relationships and themselves. Because splitting is the signature defence of BPD, it is the correct answer, and it is a frequent clinical and exam association.", "topic": "Splitting in borderline personality disorder"}, {"q": "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are:", "options": {"a": "Sympathetic and parasympathetic", "b": "Central and peripheral", "c": "Voluntary and involuntary", "d": "Cerebrospinal and cortical"}, "correct": "a", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is sympathetic and parasympathetic because these are the two divisions of the autonomic nervous system. To get this right you must hold the full architecture of the nervous system in mind. The nervous system splits first into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside it). The peripheral nervous system then divides into the somatic nervous system, which carries sensory information in and controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement, and the autonomic nervous system, which controls involuntary functions of internal organs, glands, and smooth muscle (heart rate, digestion, pupil size, sweating). The autonomic nervous system in turn has two branches that generally oppose and balance each other. The sympathetic division mobilizes the body for fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon): it increases heart rate, dilates pupils, redirects blood to muscles, and releases stored energy during stress or emergency. The parasympathetic division promotes rest-and-digest: it conserves energy, slows the heart, and supports digestion and recovery once the threat passes. So the autonomic nervous system equals sympathetic plus parasympathetic. The trap options describe other valid nervous-system distinctions but at the wrong level: central versus peripheral is the top-level split of the whole nervous system, and voluntary versus involuntary describes the somatic versus autonomic distinction, not the parts within the autonomic system itself. Keep the hierarchy clean and the two divisions, sympathetic and parasympathetic, follow immediately.", "topic": "Sympathetic and parasympathetic divisions"}, {"q": "Principle of identity in early childhood refers to", "options": {"a": "the ability to recognize positive characteristics about oneself", "b": "the ability to recognize that certain characteristics of objects remain fixed even when the appearance of the object changes.", "c": "the ability to identify with one's gender.", "d": "the ability to regulate behaviour and attitudes so that they are in harmony with that of the world."}, "correct": "b", "section": "dev", "explanation": "In Jean Piaget's account of cognitive development, summarised by Jean Piaget and Barbel Inhelder in The Psychology of the Child (English edition 1969), the preoperational child (about 2 to 7 years) gradually grasps identity: the understanding that people and things keep their basic nature even when their form, size or appearance changes. Example: Kabir, aged five, knows the clay is still the same clay after his sister rolls it into a sausage, and that his father in a tiger mask is still his father. Piaget distinguished this qualitative identity (it is the same thing) from conservation, which is quantitative invariance (it is the same amount). A preoperational child may agree that water poured into a tall thin glass is the same water yet insist there is now more of it, because centration fixes attention on the height. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) children justify conservation with three arguments: identity (nothing was added or taken away), reversibility (pour it back and it will be the same) and compensation (taller but thinner). Conservation is mastered unevenly across materials, a pattern Piaget called horizontal decalage: number first, then mass, then weight, then volume. Do not confuse this principle with gender identity, with ego identity in Erik Erikson's (1950) adolescent stage, or with the self-concept. Option b is right because the principle of identity is the recognition that an object's essential characteristics stay fixed even when its appearance changes.", "topic": "Piaget identity principle"}, {"q": "A criticism of the REBT cognitive approach to understanding mental-health problems is that:", "options": {"a": "There is too much of a focus on sexuality", "b": "It can be direct and confrontational and so might be seen as intimidatory by the client", "c": "It has not been shown to be successful at helping individuals with depression", "d": "It is not relevant in the 21st century"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is that it can be direct and confrontational and so might be seen as intimidatory by the client. Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), developed by Albert Ellis, is an active, directive form of cognitive therapy in which the therapist works vigorously to identify and dispute the client's irrational beliefs (such as absolutistic 'musts' and 'shoulds', catastrophising, and self-downing) and to replace them with more rational, flexible beliefs. A recognised criticism of this style is precisely its forcefulness: because Ellis advocated an energetic, sometimes blunt and confrontational approach to challenging irrational thinking, some clients may experience the therapist's disputing as harsh, intimidating, or uncomfortable, and a strong therapeutic alliance can be harder to build, particularly for vulnerable or fragile clients. This contrasts with the gentle, non-directive warmth of Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy. The other options are inaccurate: an over-emphasis on sexuality is a criticism levelled at Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis rather than REBT (option A); REBT and cognitive-behavioural therapy in general have a strong evidence base for treating depression and anxiety, so option C is false; and REBT remains widely practised and influential today, so option D is false. This item tests awareness of the confrontational-style criticism specific to REBT.", "topic": "Criticism of REBT confrontational style", "fig": "Albert Ellis"}, {"q": "Schizophrenia is most commonly found in :", "options": {"a": "Adolescents", "b": "Young Adults", "c": "The Middle-Aged", "d": "The Elderly"}, "correct": "b", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The correct answer is young adults. Schizophrenia is a severe psychotic disorder characterized by positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech), negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition, alogia), and cognitive impairment, described in modern nosology by the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association and historically named by Eugen Bleuler, who coined schizophrenia, building on the dementia praecox concept of Emil Kraepelin. Its typical age of onset falls in late adolescence to early adulthood, most commonly the late teens to mid-twenties for men and roughly the mid-twenties to early thirties for women, with women showing a smaller second peak around midlife. Thus the disorder is most commonly found in young adults. The relatively early onset reflects the neurodevelopmental nature of the illness, in which subtle brain maturation abnormalities interact with the demands and stresses of the transition to adulthood, consistent with the diathesis-stress and dopamine hypotheses of schizophrenia. Onset in childhood or in the elderly does occur but is far less common. For the exam, the key fact is that the peak incidence and most typical presentation cluster in young adulthood.", "topic": "Typical age of schizophrenia onset"}, {"q": "Experimental designs with more than one independent variable, in which the different variable levels are combined in a systematic manner, are:", "options": {"a": "Factorial Experiments", "b": "Quasi Experiments", "c": "Systematic Experiments", "d": "Descriptive Experiments"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "Experimental designs with more than one independent variable whose levels are systematically combined are factorial experiments, so the answer is a. A factorial design is one in which two or more independent variables (called factors) are studied simultaneously, and every level of each factor is crossed with every level of the other factors, so that all possible combinations of conditions are tested. For example, a 2 by 3 factorial crosses two levels of one factor with three levels of another, yielding six conditions. The great advantage of factorial designs is efficiency and, crucially, the ability to detect interaction effects, that is, whether the effect of one independent variable depends on the level of another, in addition to the separate main effects of each variable. Interactions are often the most theoretically interesting findings because they reveal that variables do not act in isolation but combine in complex ways. The systematic combination of variable levels mentioned in the question is the defining feature of a factorial design. The exam discriminator is matching more than one independent variable, with levels combined systematically, to factorial experiments, distinguishing them from quasi experiments (which lack random assignment), and from the non standard labels in the other options.", "topic": "Factorial experimental design"}, {"q": "Which psychological approach emphasizes learning through stimulus-response associations, mainly via conditioning?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive approach", "b": "Stimulus-Response Behaviouristic approach", "c": "Psychoanalytic approach", "d": "Existential approach"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "Behaviorism is the school of psychology that restricts explanation to observable stimuli and responses, deliberately excluding introspection and unobservable mental events. Its stimulus-response (S-R) framework holds that behavior is acquired through conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, pairs a neutral stimulus with a reflex-eliciting one until the neutral stimulus alone evokes the response. John B. Watson, who wrote the behaviorist manifesto in 1913, applied this to human emotion in the Little Albert study. Operant conditioning, formulated by B. F. Skinner building on the law of effect of Edward Thorndike, explains how consequences (reinforcement and punishment) shape voluntary behavior. All of these treat learning as the formation of associations between stimuli and responses. The cognitive approach (option a) arose partly in reaction to behaviorism, insisting that internal representations mediate behavior; the psychoanalytic (c) and existential (d) approaches address unconscious conflict and meaning respectively. Because option b names the stimulus-response behaviorist approach explicitly, it is correct.", "topic": "Behaviourism S-R approach", "fig": "John B. Watson"}, {"q": "Summated rating scale is also known as :", "options": {"a": "Likert scale", "b": "Thurstone scale", "c": "Guttmann scale", "d": "Equal appearing interval scale"}, "correct": "a", "section": "assessment", "explanation": "The correct answer is the Likert scale. A summated rating scale is an attitude-measurement scale in which a respondent indicates their degree of agreement with a set of statements along an ordered response continuum (for example, strongly disagree, disagree, neutral, agree, strongly agree, typically scored 1 to 5), and the responses across all items are added or averaged to yield a total score representing the person's overall attitude, hence summated. This method was devised by Rensis Likert in 1932, so the summated rating scale is synonymous with the Likert scale. Its advantages are ease of construction, reliability, and the ability to measure intensity of feeling. It is important to distinguish it from other classic attitude scales. The Thurstone scale, developed by Louis Leon Thurstone, is the method of equal-appearing intervals, in which judges first assign scale values to statements and respondents endorse those they agree with; option d (equal appearing interval scale) is therefore another name for the Thurstone scale, not the Likert scale. The Guttman scale, developed by Louis Guttman, is a cumulative scale in which agreeing with a stronger item implies agreement with all weaker ones, producing a unidimensional ordering. Because the summated rating method is Likert's, option a is correct.", "topic": "Likert summated rating scale", "fig": "Rensis Likert"}, {"q": "Whether or not disadvantaged, people try to change what is perceived as unfair social structure depends on:", "options": {"a": "Stability and sociability of the social structure", "b": "Morality and permeability of the social structure", "c": "Permeability and stability of the social structure", "d": "Competence and morality of the social structure"}, "correct": "c", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is c: whether members of a disadvantaged group try to change an unfair social structure depends on the permeability and stability of that structure. Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979), in social identity theory, argued that people seek a positive social identity from the groups they belong to. When their group compares badly with others, two features of the status system decide how they respond. Permeability is whether group boundaries can be crossed: if an individual can move up into the higher-status group, people choose social mobility, leaving the group as individuals instead of challenging the system. Stability is whether the status order looks fixed or open to change, and it works together with legitimacy (whether the order looks fair): if boundaries are closed and the hierarchy looks unstable and illegitimate, so that a different order can be imagined, people turn to social competition, collectively challenging the dominant group. If the hierarchy looks stable and legitimate, they fall back on social creativity, comparing on a new dimension ('we are poorer but kinder'). Example: Kabir studies at a low-ranked college. If good marks let him transfer to a top college (permeable boundaries), he works for his own transfer; if transfers are closed but the ranking system looks unfair and open to challenge (unstable), his classmates organise together to demand recognition. Option c is right because permeability and stability are exactly the two structural variables the theory names. Exam trap: the distractors borrow dimensions from stereotype research, so they sound scholarly; warmth (sociability) and competence are the two dimensions of the stereotype content model, and morality is a dimension on which groups are judged, not a property of the social structure.", "topic": "Social identity: permeability and stability", "fig": "Henri Tajfel and John Turner"}, {"q": "A person, who believes that he has control over events in his life, has what type of locus of control?", "options": {"a": "External", "b": "Internal", "c": "Neutral", "d": "Situational"}, "correct": "b", "section": "personality", "explanation": "Locus of control is a personality construct introduced by Julian Rotter within his social learning theory. It describes the degree to which people believe they, as opposed to external forces, control the outcomes of events in their lives. A person with an internal locus of control believes that outcomes are contingent on their own effort, ability, and decisions, so they tend to take responsibility, persist at tasks, and show greater self-efficacy and achievement motivation. A person with an external locus of control believes that outcomes are governed by luck, fate, chance, or powerful others, and may show more passivity, learned helplessness (Martin Seligman and Steven Maier, 1967), or lower stress-coping when facing difficulties. The construct is measured by Rotter's I-E Scale and relates conceptually to attribution theory and to the self-efficacy work of Albert Bandura. Options c (neutral) and d (situational) are not genuine categories in Rotter's dichotomy. Because believing one has control over one's own life events is the defining feature of an internal locus of control, option b is correct.", "topic": "Rotter internal locus of control", "fig": "Julian Rotter"}, {"q": "The neurotransmitters called feel-good hormones are known as:", "options": {"a": "Dopamine", "b": "Endorphins", "c": "GABA", "d": "Acetylcholine"}, "correct": "b", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The correct answer is endorphins. Endorphins (the name is a contraction of 'endogenous morphine') are the body's natural opioid neuropeptides, popularly called the feel-good chemicals because they produce feelings of pleasure, euphoria, and pain relief. They are released by the pituitary gland and the central nervous system in response to stress, pain, exercise, laughter, and certain foods, and they bind to the same opioid receptors that morphine and other opiate drugs act on. By binding to these receptors, endorphins inhibit the transmission of pain signals and trigger a sense of well-being, which is the mechanism behind phenomena such as the runner's high, the analgesia of intense exercise or acupuncture, and stress-induced pain suppression. Beta-endorphin is the most potent of these peptides. Physiologically they form part of the body's intrinsic pain-modulation system, acting in regions such as the periaqueductal grey to dampen ascending pain pathways. While dopamine is also strongly tied to reward and pleasure, the specific phrase feel-good hormones in this question maps to endorphins, given their direct role in producing euphoria and natural pain relief. Understanding endorphins links the psychology of mood and pain to the body's own opioid chemistry.", "topic": "Endorphins as endogenous opioids"}, {"q": "A child's increasing skill at using his muscles is because of:", "options": {"a": "An opportunity to exercise", "b": "Pushing by parents", "c": "Learning", "d": "Maturation"}, "correct": "d", "section": "dev", "explanation": "The correct answer is maturation, the genetically programmed, biologically driven sequence of physical and neuromuscular development that unfolds with age relatively independently of specific experience. A child's growing skill at controlling the muscles, such as the orderly progression from sitting to crawling to standing to walking and then to fine motor control, depends primarily on the maturation of the nervous system and musculoskeletal system, especially the myelination of nerve fibers and the development of motor pathways, rather than on practice or teaching. The classic evidence comes from Arnold Gesell, whose co-twin control studies showed that a twin given early training in skills like stair-climbing gained little lasting advantage over the untrained twin once the latter reached the maturational readiness to perform the skill, demonstrating that readiness, not practice, sets the pace. This illustrates the broader developmental concept of the interaction between maturation and learning: maturation provides the biological readiness that makes a skill possible, while learning and experience refine and elaborate it, but the emergence of basic motor competence is maturationally governed. The principle to learn cold is that motor milestones appear in a universal, predictable cephalocaudal (head-to-toe) and proximodistal (center-outward) sequence across cultures and child-rearing practices precisely because they are maturation-driven, which is why pushing or extra exercise cannot meaningfully accelerate a child beyond their maturational readiness.", "topic": "Maturation in motor development"}, {"q": "In Reality Therapy, developed by William Glasser, which of the following is considered one of the five basic genetic needs?", "options": {"a": "Self-actualization", "b": "Power or Achievement", "c": "Collective Unconscious", "d": "Unconditional positive regard"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is power or achievement because it is one of the five basic genetic needs in William Glasser's Reality Therapy and its underlying Choice Theory. Glasser, an American psychiatrist, argued that human behaviour is not driven by external circumstances or the past but is chosen in the present as our best attempt to satisfy five fundamental, biologically built-in needs. These five needs are survival (the physiological need for food, shelter, safety, and health), love and belonging (connection, relationships, and being part of a group), power or achievement (a sense of competence, accomplishment, recognition, and self-worth), freedom (autonomy and independence, the ability to make one's own choices), and fun (enjoyment, pleasure, and play). Glasser held that psychological problems typically arise from difficulties in relationships and from unmet needs, and that Reality Therapy helps clients evaluate whether their current choices are effectively meeting these needs, then make more responsible, effective choices in the here and now, taking personal responsibility rather than blaming the past. The distractors are famous concepts from other theorists deliberately placed to test whether you can keep the frameworks separate: self-actualization belongs to Abraham Maslow, the collective unconscious to Carl Jung, and unconditional positive regard to Carl Rogers. Only power or achievement is genuinely one of Glasser's five basic needs, making it the correct answer.", "topic": "Glasser choice theory basic needs", "fig": "William Glasser"}, {"q": "Schizophrenia corresponds to the autistic phase of development and the person fails to develop an adequate and stable symbiosis, was the developmental theory of schizophrenia given by:", "options": {"a": "Ping-Nie Pao", "b": "Sylvano Arieti", "c": "Margaret Mahler", "d": "Edith Jacobson"}, "correct": "c", "section": "disorders", "explanation": "The developmental theory that schizophrenia corresponds to the autistic phase and a failure to develop stable symbiosis was given by Margaret Mahler, so the answer is c. Mahler was a psychoanalyst who studied the development of the self in infancy through her separation individuation theory. She proposed that the infant moves through phases: an early normal autistic phase (the first weeks, a self absorbed, undifferentiated state with little awareness of the outside world), followed by the normal symbiotic phase (around the second to fifth month, in which the infant experiences a fused, dual unity with the mother as if they were one boundaryless system), and then the separation individuation process, with its subphases of differentiation, practising, rapprochement, and consolidation of individuality. Mahler theorised that severe psychopathology reflects fixation at or failure to negotiate these earliest phases. In her view, the most disturbed conditions correspond to the earliest phases: psychotic disturbance is linked to a failure to move beyond the autistic and symbiotic phases, with schizophrenia reflecting the autistic phase and an inability to form an adequate, stable symbiosis with the mother, leaving the self without a secure foundation. The exam discriminator is the specific Mahlerian language of autistic and symbiotic phases and separation individuation, which distinguishes her account from those of Pao, Arieti, and Jacobson.", "topic": "Mahler autistic symbiotic theory of schizophrenia", "fig": "Margaret Mahler"}, {"q": "Which scale of measurement involves categories without any order or ranking, such as car colors?", "options": {"a": "Nominal scale", "b": "Ordinal scale", "c": "Interval scale", "d": "Ratio scale"}, "correct": "a", "section": "research", "explanation": "The four levels of measurement were formalized by the psychophysicist Stanley Smith Stevens in 1946, arranged in increasing order of mathematical sophistication: nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio. A nominal scale is the most basic: it classifies data into distinct categories that have no inherent order, rank, or quantitative relationship. The categories merely name or label groups, such as car colors, gender, blood type, or nationality; numbers, if assigned, are just identifiers and cannot be meaningfully compared as greater or less. An ordinal scale adds rank order (for example, first, second, third, or a Likert agreement rating) but without equal intervals between ranks. An interval scale adds equal, meaningful intervals but has an arbitrary zero (for example, temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale adds a true, absolute zero, permitting statements about ratios (for example, weight, height, or reaction time). Because unordered categories such as car colors are the defining feature of the nominal scale, option a is correct, while the distractors describe higher, ordered levels of measurement.", "topic": "Nominal scale of measurement"}, {"q": "Charles Spearman is to Factor Analysis as Wilhelm Wundt is to:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Introspection", "c": "Functionalism", "d": "Humanism"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The answer is introspection, because the analogy pairs each pioneer with the method he is famous for. Charles Spearman invented factor analysis, the statistical technique of extracting underlying dimensions from correlations, and used it to propose his two-factor theory of intelligence with the general factor g. The analogy therefore asks: what method is Wilhelm Wundt's signature? Wundt founded the first formal psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, the event conventionally treated as the birth of scientific psychology, and his method was introspection, sometimes called experimental self-observation. Trained observers were exposed to carefully controlled stimuli, such as a metronome beat or a light, and reported the elementary sensations and feelings in their conscious experience. The aim was to analyze consciousness into its basic elements, the program his student Edward Titchener later systematized in America as structuralism. Grasp why the distractors fail by attaching each school to its founder: psychoanalysis belongs to Sigmund Freud, functionalism to William James and the Darwinian question of what consciousness does, and humanism to Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow. Analogy questions like this reward a simple study habit: for every historical figure, memorize one method and one school. Spearman goes with factor analysis and g; Wundt goes with introspection and the Leipzig laboratory; that pairing settles the item instantly.", "topic": "Wundt and introspection method", "fig": "Wilhelm Wundt"}, {"q": "The phenomenon where a qualified individual is prevented from advancing to higher positions in their career due to an invisible barrier based on gender or minority status is called:", "options": {"a": "The Matthew Effect", "b": "The Glass Ceiling Effect", "c": "The Halo Effect", "d": "The Pygmalion Effect"}, "correct": "b", "section": "social", "explanation": "The answer is the glass ceiling effect, because an invisible, unacknowledged barrier that prevents qualified individuals, particularly women and minorities, from advancing to higher-level positions despite their qualifications is precisely what the term glass ceiling denotes. Understand the metaphor and the concept behind it. The phrase glass ceiling became popular in the 1980s to describe a barrier that is transparent, meaning it is not written into any official policy and the higher positions are visibly within sight, yet real and impenetrable, so that women and members of minority groups can see the top ranks but cannot reach them because of subtle, systemic, often unconscious discrimination, biases, and organizational cultures that block their promotion. The barrier is invisible because it operates through informal networks, stereotypes about leadership, lack of mentorship, and biased evaluation rather than through explicit rules, which makes it harder to identify and challenge. The concept is central to organizational psychology and the study of gender and workplace inequality, and it has spawned related terms such as the glass cliff (women being promoted to leadership in times of crisis when failure is likely) and the sticky floor (forces keeping women in low-level jobs). Contrast it with the distractors: the Matthew effect is about cumulative advantage, the halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is an impression-formation bias, and the Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is an expectancy-driven self-fulfilling prophecy. The specific idea of an invisible barrier to advancement rooted in gender or minority status is the glass ceiling effect.", "topic": "Glass ceiling effect"}, {"q": "The Resting Membrane Potential is:", "options": {"a": "-40 mV", "b": "+40 mV", "c": "-70 mV to -80 mV", "d": "+70 mV"}, "correct": "c", "section": "physio", "explanation": "The answer is -70 mV to -80 mV because the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron, the steady electrical charge difference across its membrane when it is not firing, is about -70 millivolts (commonly cited in the -65 to -80 mV range), with the inside of the cell negative relative to the outside. This polarized state exists because of an uneven distribution of ions. Inside the neuron there is a high concentration of potassium ions and large negatively charged proteins, while outside there is a high concentration of sodium and chloride ions. Two forces maintain the resting potential: selective membrane permeability and the sodium-potassium pump. At rest the membrane is far more permeable to potassium than to sodium, so potassium leaks out down its concentration gradient, leaving the interior negative. The sodium-potassium pump actively transports three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in, using ATP, which both maintains the gradients and contributes slightly to the negativity. This resting state is essential because it sets up the potential energy that, when a stimulus opens sodium channels, allows rapid depolarization and the firing of an action potential, which peaks near +30 to +40 mV before repolarizing. So while +40 mV (option b) describes the approximate peak of the action potential, the resting value the question asks for is the negative -70 to -80 mV range.", "topic": "Resting membrane potential -70 mV"}, {"q": "Biopsychosocial model of illness was proposed by", "options": {"a": "Engel, G.L.", "b": "Freud, S.", "c": "Cannon, W.B.", "d": "Lazarus, R.S."}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The biopsychosocial model was proposed by the psychiatrist George L. Engel in a landmark 1977 Science paper. Engel argued that the prevailing biomedical model, which reduced illness to deranged biochemistry and physiology, was too narrow to explain why people become sick, how they experience suffering, and what helps them recover. His model insists that health and illness emerge from the interaction of three levels: biological factors (genetics, pathogens, neurochemistry), psychological factors (beliefs, emotions, coping, behaviour), and social factors (family, culture, socioeconomic status, access to care). Drawing loosely on general systems theory, Engel framed the person as one level in a nested hierarchy running from molecules and cells up to the family, community, and society. The practical upshot is that a good clinician asks not only what is biologically wrong but also how the patient understands the illness and what social circumstances shape its course. This model became the philosophical backbone of health psychology and of modern integrated care. Contrast it with the distractors: Freud's psychoanalysis, Cannon's homeostasis and fight-or-flight physiology, and Lazarus's transactional stress-and-appraisal theory are each important, but none is the tri-level illness framework the question describes. Whenever a stem pairs biological, psychological, and social explicitly, the name to reach for is Engel.", "topic": "Engel biopsychosocial model", "fig": "George Engel"}, {"q": "The Stroop effect demonstrates interference in", "options": {"a": "Long-term memory retrieval.", "b": "Working memory.", "c": "Selective attention.", "d": "Procedural memory."}, "correct": "c", "section": "general", "explanation": "The Stroop effect, first reported by John Ridley Stroop in 1935, appears when you are shown a colour word printed in a conflicting ink colour (for example the word RED printed in blue) and are asked to name the ink colour, not read the word. People are reliably slower and more error-prone here than when word and colour match. The reason is that reading is a highly automatic, over-learned process, so the word meaning is processed involuntarily and competes with the deliberate, effortful task of naming the ink. To respond correctly you must suppress the dominant reading response and select the relevant dimension (colour) while ignoring the irrelevant one (meaning). That act of prioritising one source of information and filtering out a competing one is exactly what selective attention does. The interference reflects the cost of resolving this attentional conflict, which is why the Stroop task is a classic measure of attentional control and, in clinical work, of executive dysfunction. It taps the mechanism that lets us focus on goal-relevant input despite distraction, rather than any failure of storage or retrieval. Modern accounts tie it to conflict monitoring by the anterior cingulate cortex, but the core phenomenon it demonstrates is interference within selective attention.", "topic": "Stroop effect selective attention", "fig": "John Ridley Stroop"}, {"q": "Noogenic anxiety is described under:", "options": {"a": "Humanistic School", "b": "Gestalt school", "c": "Existential school", "d": "Psychodynamic school"}, "correct": "c", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "Noogenic anxiety (and noogenic neurosis) is described under the existential school, so the answer is c. The concept comes from Viktor Frankl, the founder of logotherapy, which is often grouped within existential therapy because of its focus on meaning, freedom, and responsibility. Frankl coined the term noogenic, from the Greek noos meaning mind or spirit, to denote disturbances that arise not from psychological conflict in the Freudian sense or from biological causes, but from the spiritual or existential dimension of the person, specifically from a frustration of the will to meaning. Noogenic neurosis and noogenic anxiety stem from an existential vacuum, the experience of emptiness, meaninglessness, and the loss of purpose, and from conflicts of conscience and values rather than from drive conflicts. Frankl argued that the central human motivation is the search for meaning, and that when this striving is thwarted, a distinctively existential form of distress arises that ordinary psychotherapy aimed at drives or symptoms cannot reach; logotherapy addresses it by helping the person discover meaning. The exam discriminator is the link between noogenic, the will to meaning, the existential vacuum, and Frankl, all of which place the concept in the existential school rather than the humanistic, Gestalt, or psychodynamic traditions.", "topic": "Frankl noogenic neurosis existential", "fig": "Viktor Frankl"}, {"q": "Learned helplessness phenomenon is offered by", "options": {"a": "Aron T. Beck.", "b": "Martin Seligmam and Steven F. Maier.", "c": "Harry Stack Sullivan.", "d": "Daniel Kahneman."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "Learned helplessness is the passivity and failure to escape that follow exposure to aversive events the organism cannot control. It was demonstrated by Martin E. P. Seligman and Steven F. Maier (1967) and by J. Bruce Overmier and Seligman (1967). In the triadic design, dogs in a harness received escapable shock (pressing a panel ended it), inescapable shock yoked to the first group so the amount was identical, or no shock. Later all dogs were placed in a shuttle box where jumping a low barrier ended the shock. Most dogs from the escapable and no-shock groups learned quickly; most yoked dogs lay down and whined, failing to escape. Because shock amount was matched, the deficit came from lack of control, not shock itself. Seligman (1975) proposed this as a model of depression. The original model could not explain why human helplessness is sometimes brief and narrow, sometimes lasting and general, so Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated it in attributional terms: depression follows when bad events are explained by internal, stable and global causes. Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989) extended this into hopelessness theory. Neuroscience led Maier and Seligman (2016) to argue that passivity is the default response to prolonged aversive events and that what is learned is control, detected by the medial prefrontal cortex. Worked example: Rohan, after repeated failure in maths despite effort, stops trying even when a new teacher makes success possible. Option b is right because Seligman and Maier are the researchers who demonstrated learned helplessness.", "topic": "Seligman learned helplessness", "fig": "Martin Seligman and Steven Maier"}, {"q": "Fixed role therapy, which encouraged clients to practice new roles and relationships, was developed by:", "options": {"a": "George Kelly", "b": "Aaron Beck", "c": "Albert Ellis", "d": "Fritz Perls"}, "correct": "a", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "Fixed role therapy was developed by George Kelly, so the answer is a. George Kelly was the founder of personal construct theory, set out in his 1955 work The Psychology of Personal Constructs. His central idea was that people are like naive scientists who anticipate and make sense of the world through bipolar personal constructs, the contrasting dimensions such as friendly versus hostile that they use to interpret events. Psychological problems arise when a person's construct system is too rigid, too constricted, or fails to predict experience well. Fixed role therapy is the clever therapeutic application of this idea. The therapist, often after a self characterisation sketch written by the client, drafts a fixed role sketch describing a hypothetical character who construes the world somewhat differently, in healthier or more flexible ways. The client is then invited to enact and live out this new role for a set period, as if trying on a different identity in an experiment. By behaving as the new character, the client gathers fresh evidence, loosens fixed constructs, and discovers that alternative ways of construing and acting are possible, which reflects Kelly's founding principle of constructive alternativism, that any event can be construed in more than one way. The discriminating cue, practising new roles and relationships within a constructivist frame, points specifically to Kelly, distinguishing his approach from the cognitive and gestalt therapies of the other options.", "topic": "Kelly fixed role therapy", "fig": "George Kelly"}, {"q": "The most important school of Psychology which has contributed a lot towards perception is:", "options": {"a": "Psychoanalysis", "b": "Behaviour school", "c": "Structural school", "d": "Gestalt psychology"}, "correct": "d", "section": "general", "explanation": "Gestalt psychology is the school that contributed most to the study of perception. Founded in Germany around 1912 by Max Wertheimer, with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as its other principal figures, Gestalt psychology arose as a direct reaction against the structuralism of Wundt and Titchener, which tried to break consciousness down into elementary sensations. Wertheimer's key demonstration was the phi phenomenon: two stationary lights flashed in quick succession are perceived as a single light moving, an experience that cannot be reduced to the individual sensations and proves that the perceived whole differs from the sum of its parts. From this came the movement's famous dictum that 'the whole is other than the sum of its parts.' Gestalt theorists then formulated the laws of perceptual organization, the principles by which the mind groups sensory elements into coherent wholes: proximity (near things group together), similarity (like things group together), closure (we mentally complete incomplete figures), continuity (we perceive smooth continuous forms), and figure-ground segregation (we separate an object from its background). These laws remain foundational in the psychology of perception today. Because no other school built such a systematic account of how raw sensation becomes organized perception, Gestalt is the correct answer.", "topic": "Gestalt school and perception"}, {"q": "In existential psychology, Dasein encompasses three fundamental psychological dimensions of human existence. Which of the following correctly identifies these three modes of being-in-the-world?", "options": {"a": "Id, Ego, and Superego", "b": "Umwelt, Mitwelt, and Eigenwelt", "c": "Conscious, Preconscious, and Unconscious", "d": "Sensorimotor, Preoperational, and Concrete Operational"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is Umwelt, Mitwelt, and Eigenwelt because in existential psychology, particularly as elaborated by Ludwig Binswanger and Medard Boss and popularised for English readers by Rollo May, human existence (Dasein) is understood as simultaneously unfolding across three inseparable modes or dimensions of being-in-the-world. The first is the Umwelt, literally the 'around-world', meaning the physical or natural world of our biology and environment, the world of drives, instincts, and the material surroundings we share with all living things. The second is the Mitwelt, the 'with-world', meaning the social world of our relationships and interactions with other people, the realm of community, love, and human connection. The third is the Eigenwelt, the 'own-world', meaning the private inner world of one's own self, one's relationship to oneself, self-awareness, and personal identity, a dimension existentialists argued is often neglected. A psychologically whole person lives authentically across all three at once, and disturbance can be understood as an imbalance or breakdown in one of them. The trap in this item is that all four options list a famous triad, so you must attach each triad to its owner: Freud's structural model (id, ego, superego), Freud's topographic model (conscious, preconscious, unconscious), Piaget's cognitive stages, and the existential world-modes. Only the German-rooted Umwelt, Mitwelt, and Eigenwelt belong to the existential analysis of Dasein.", "topic": "Umwelt Mitwelt Eigenwelt modes", "fig": "Ludwig Binswanger"}, {"q": "Which psychologist is associated with the concept of \"affordances\", which refers to the opportunities for action provided by the environment?", "options": {"a": "Hermann von Helmholtz", "b": "James J. Gibson", "c": "Richard Gregory", "d": "Ulric Neisser"}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "The correct answer is James J. Gibson. James J. Gibson coined the term affordances to describe the action possibilities that the environment offers an organism: a chair affords sitting, a handle affords grasping, a surface affords walking. Affordances are a cornerstone of his ecological theory of direct perception, which argues that the environment presents rich, invariant information that organisms perceive directly, without the need for internal inference or mental construction. Perception, for Gibson, is geared to action; we perceive what we can do with objects. Hence option B is correct. Hermann von Helmholtz (option A) is associated with the very different idea of unconscious inference, a foundation of the constructivist view that perception is an inferential best guess, which contrasts with Gibson's direct perception. Richard Gregory (option C) was a leading proponent of that constructivist, top-down, hypothesis-testing account, essentially the opposing camp to Gibson's, so he is not linked to affordances. Ulric Neisser (option D) is often called the father of cognitive psychology for his 1967 book Cognitive Psychology, and he later advocated an ecological approach to memory, but the specific concept of affordances belongs to Gibson. The notion of affordances has since become influential well beyond perception, notably in design and human-computer interaction.", "topic": "Gibson affordances", "fig": "James J. Gibson"}, {"q": "Who developed the technique of Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT)?", "options": {"a": "L. Benjamin", "b": "Peter Fonagy and A. Bateman", "c": "Otto F. Kernberg", "d": "G.L. Klerman and M. M. Weissman"}, "correct": "d", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The correct answer is Gerald L. Klerman and Myrna M. Weissman. Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) is a structured, time-limited, evidence-based psychotherapy developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (with colleagues) initially for the treatment of major depression. Its central premise is that psychological symptoms, particularly depression, arise and are maintained within an interpersonal context, so improving the person's current relationships and social functioning relieves the symptoms. Rather than focusing on unconscious conflict or on distorted cognitions, IPT concentrates on the here-and-now and targets one or more of four problem areas linked to the onset of the episode: grief (complicated bereavement), interpersonal role disputes (conflicts with significant others), role transitions (major life changes such as divorce, retirement, or a new job), and interpersonal deficits (a history of impoverished or unsatisfying relationships). Its theoretical roots draw on the interpersonal school of Harry Stack Sullivan and on attachment theory from John Bowlby. IPT has strong empirical support for depression and has been adapted for other conditions. The distractors name other clinicians and their distinct therapies: Lorna Smith Benjamin (SASB), Peter Fonagy and Anthony Bateman (mentalization-based treatment), and Otto Kernberg (transference-focused psychotherapy). Because IPT was developed by Klerman and Weissman, option d is correct.", "topic": "Interpersonal psychotherapy developers", "fig": "Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman"}, {"q": "According to Baddeley's Model of Working Memory, the phonological loop is responsible for", "options": {"a": "Visual-spatial information.", "b": "Auditory-verbal information.", "c": "Procedural memory.", "d": "Long term memory consolidation."}, "correct": "b", "section": "general", "explanation": "Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposed the multi-component working memory model in 1974 to replace the older single short-term store. It has a central executive that allocates attention, supervised by two subordinate 'slave' systems, with an episodic buffer added later. The phonological loop is the speech-based system: it holds acoustic and verbal information for a few seconds. It has two parts, a phonological store (the 'inner ear' that briefly holds sound-based traces) and an articulatory rehearsal process (the 'inner voice' that refreshes those traces by silent repetition). This is why you can hold a phone number by repeating it to yourself, and why similar-sounding items are harder to recall (the phonological similarity effect). The visuospatial sketchpad, by contrast, handles visual and spatial imagery, and procedural memory belongs to Squire's long-term implicit systems. So the phonological loop is specifically responsible for auditory-verbal material. Remember the mnemonic: loop is for language and sound, sketchpad is for sights and space. Baddeley's model matters clinically because deficits in the loop appear in specific language impairment and some learning disabilities, while executive dysfunction points more to the central executive. Keeping the slave systems straight is a favourite exam distinction, so anchor the loop firmly to the ear and voice.", "topic": "Phonological loop function", "fig": "Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch"}, {"q": "The concept of \"self story\" or helping clients re-author their life narratives is most closely associated with which therapeutic approach?", "options": {"a": "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy", "b": "Narrative Therapy", "c": "Psychodynamic Therapy", "d": "Gestalt Therapy"}, "correct": "b", "section": "clinical", "explanation": "The answer is Narrative Therapy because its central, defining idea is that people make sense of their lives through the stories they tell about themselves, and that therapy helps clients re-author or re-story those narratives in more empowering ways. Narrative Therapy was developed in the 1980s by the Australian social worker Michael White and the New Zealand therapist David Epston. Its core assumption is social constructionist: our identities are not fixed inner essences but are constructed through the dominant stories, shaped by culture, family, and language, that we come to believe about ourselves. When a person is dominated by a 'problem-saturated' story ('I am a failure', 'I am an anxious person'), that narrative filters their experience and traps them. The therapist's job is to help the client author a richer, more hopeful alternative story. A signature technique is externalization, captured in the phrase 'the person is not the problem, the problem is the problem', which separates the person from the difficulty (talking about 'the Anxiety' as an external influence rather than an identity) so the client can resist it. Other techniques include finding unique outcomes or exceptions that contradict the problem story, and thickening the new preferred narrative. Because re-authoring the self-story is the hallmark of White and Epston's approach, the answer is Narrative Therapy.", "topic": "Narrative therapy re-authoring", "fig": "Michael White and David Epston"}, {"q": "The capacity of working memory is best-described in terms of number of meaningful units of information, called", "options": {"a": "Chunks", "b": "Features", "c": "Scripts", "d": "Proposition"}, "correct": "a", "section": "general", "explanation": "Working memory (and its predecessor concept, short-term memory) holds a very limited amount of information for immediate use. George A. Miller captured this in his famous 1956 paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two,' proposing that the span of immediate memory is roughly seven items. Crucially, Miller argued that the limiting factor is not the number of raw stimuli but the number of meaningful units, which he called chunks. A chunk is a cluster of elements bound together into a single familiar unit through knowledge and experience. For instance, the string F-B-I-C-I-A is six letters but only two chunks if you recognize the agencies, letting you hold far more raw letters than the seven-item limit would otherwise allow. Chunking is why an expert can recall long strings in their domain: they recode many small elements into fewer high-order units. This reframes capacity as a matter of organization and prior knowledge, not sheer sensory load, and it foreshadows later work showing capacity may be nearer four chunks (Nelson Cowan). The distractors name real cognitive constructs but from other domains: features belong to perception and pattern recognition, scripts and propositions to long-term semantic memory. When a question asks for the meaningful units that define working-memory capacity, the intended term, straight from Miller, is chunks.", "topic": "Chunking in short-term memory", "fig": "George Miller"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"diagnostic-2": [{"q": "Who gave the concept of ‘Folk psychology’?", "concept": "The correct answer is Wilhelm Maximilian Wundt. Wilhelm Wundt is famous as the founder of experimental psychology, having established the first formal psychology laboratory in Leipzig in 1879, and for trained introspection to analyse the elements of conscious experience, the program his student Edward Titchener later named structuralism and took to America. Less well known but equally important is that Wundt also developed the concept of Volkerpsychologie, usually translated as folk psychology or cultural psychology. In his later years Wundt devoted a monumental ten-volume work to this subject, arguing that the higher mental processes, those shaped by social and cultural life such as language, myth, religion, customs, and art, could not be studied through laboratory experiment and introspection alone, because they arise from collective human activity over history. Instead, these phenomena had to be understood by examining the products of communities and cultures. Thus Wundt distinguished between an experimental, physiological psychology of individual immediate experience and a folk psychology of the collective mind expressed through culture. This dual programme makes Wundt a foundational figure for both experimental and social-cultural psychology. Because the concept of Volkerpsychologie (folk psychology) was Wundt's, he is the correct answer.", "traps": {"b": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, focusing on the unconscious and psychosexual development, not folk psychology.", "c": "Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning through his work on salivation in dogs, unrelated to folk psychology.", "d": "Wilhelm Roentgen was a physicist who discovered X-rays; he was not a psychologist and the similar first name is a trap."}}, {"q": "What type of psychological test is the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS)?", "concept": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) is the most widely used individually administered intelligence test for adults. It was developed by David Wechsler, who defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally, and deal effectively with the environment. Unlike the earlier Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916), which yielded a single ratio IQ (William Stern, 1912), Wechsler's scales produce a Full Scale IQ along with index scores for distinct domains such as verbal comprehension, perceptual reasoning (split into visual spatial and fluid reasoning in the WAIS-5, 2024), working memory, and processing speed, using a deviation IQ with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. The WAIS is therefore squarely an intelligence test, measuring general cognitive ability. The distractors name other categories: an aptitude test predicts capacity for a specific future task or training, an attitude test measures evaluative opinions, and an achievement test measures knowledge already acquired in academic subjects. Because the WAIS is a standardized measure of general intelligence, option b is correct.", "traps": {"a": "The WAIS measures general cognitive ability (IQ), not aptitude for a specific future task, though intelligence tests broadly relate to aptitude; the precise category is an intelligence test.", "c": "An attitude test measures opinions and evaluations, not cognitive ability.", "d": "An achievement test measures acquired academic knowledge, not general intelligence."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a stage of group formation?", "concept": "This question refers to Bruce Tuckman's classic model of group development, published in 1965, which describes the predictable sequence teams pass through as they form and mature. The stages are, in order: Forming, in which members come together, are polite and tentative, and orient themselves to the task; Storming, in which conflict emerges as individuals assert their views, compete for roles, and test boundaries; Norming, in which the group resolves those tensions, establishes shared rules and expectations, and develops cohesion and trust; and Performing, in which the group functions smoothly and channels its energy into achieving its goals. Tuckman later added a fifth stage, Adjourning, for the disbanding of a group once its work is complete. The rhyming labels (forming, storming, norming, performing) make the sequence easy to remember and are exactly what an exam expects you to recognise. 'Proximity' is not part of this model at all; it is a concept from the psychology of attraction and Gestalt perception, referring to nearness as a factor in forming relationships or grouping stimuli. Because proximity does not belong among Tuckman's stages, it is the item that is 'not a stage of group formation', which makes it the correct answer to this negatively worded question.", "traps": {"a": "Storming is Tuckman's second stage, marked by conflict and jockeying for position within the group.", "c": "Norming is Tuckman's third stage, where the group settles on shared standards and cohesion.", "d": "Performing is Tuckman's fourth stage, when the group works effectively toward its goals."}}, {"q": "'Displacement' is a defense mechanism typically seen in:", "concept": "Displacement is a defence mechanism typically seen in phobias, so the answer is b. Displacement is the ego defence in which an emotion, impulse, or drive, often anxiety or hostility, is redirected from its original, threatening object onto a substitute object that is safer or more acceptable. In the psychoanalytic theory of phobias, the classic formulation, illustrated by Freud's case of Little Hans, holds that the child's unacceptable anxiety, originally attached to an internal conflict or a forbidden impulse toward a parent, is displaced onto an external, symbolic, and avoidable object, in Hans's case a horse. The phobic object then represents and stands in for the true, intolerable source of anxiety; by fearing and avoiding the external object, the person keeps the underlying conflict out of awareness and confines the anxiety to a circumscribed, manageable target. So phobia is understood psychodynamically as anxiety displaced onto a symbolic external stimulus. This is why displacement is the defence characteristically associated with phobic disorders. The exam discriminator is the specific psychodynamic mapping of displacement onto phobias (anxiety shifted onto an external symbolic object), distinguishing it from the defences classically tied to other conditions: conversion and repression in hysteria, the isolation undoing reaction formation triad in OCD, and introjection or turning against the self in depression.", "traps": {"a": "Hysteria (conversion disorder) is classically associated with repression and conversion of conflict into physical symptoms, not principally with displacement.", "c": "Obsessive compulsive disorder is associated with isolation, undoing, and reaction formation; displacement is not its hallmark defence.", "d": "Depression is linked psychodynamically with introjection and turning hostility against the self, not with displacement onto an external object."}}, {"q": "Which of the four lobes of the brain is responsible for interpreting sensory information?", "concept": "The correct answer is the parietal lobe. The parietal lobe, located at the top and toward the back of each cerebral hemisphere, is the brain's principal centre for integrating and interpreting somatosensory information. It contains the primary somatosensory cortex (the postcentral gyrus), which receives and processes sensations of touch, pressure, temperature, pain, and proprioception (the sense of body position) from the entire body, organised as the sensory homunculus (Wilder Penfield). Beyond raw sensation, the parietal association cortex integrates input from multiple senses to build a coherent representation of the body and of space, supporting functions such as spatial awareness, navigation, hand-eye coordination, and attention to the location of objects. Damage to the parietal lobe can produce striking deficits, including hemispatial neglect (ignoring one side of space, typically after right parietal damage), agraphesthesia (inability to recognise numbers traced on the skin), and difficulties with spatial orientation. The question asks which lobe interprets sensory information, and because the parietal lobe houses the somatosensory cortex and integrates sensory input into perception of the body and surrounding space, it is the correct choice. Each of the four lobes has a characteristic functional emphasis, and the parietal lobe's emphasis is sensory integration.", "traps": {"a": "The frontal lobe handles voluntary movement, planning, decision-making, personality, and executive function, not sensory interpretation.", "b": "The occipital lobe is dedicated to vision, processing visual input specifically rather than sensory information in general.", "d": "The temporal lobe processes hearing, language comprehension, and memory; it is not the primary site for somatosensory interpretation."}}, {"q": "According to Jean Piaget's theory of play development, what is the first stage of child play that emerges during infancy?", "concept": "The answer is functional play because in Jean Piaget's account of how play develops alongside cognition, functional play (also called practice play or sensorimotor play) is the earliest form, appearing in infancy during the sensorimotor stage. Piaget, the Swiss psychologist whose theory of cognitive development frames children as active constructors of knowledge, saw play as reflecting the child's current cognitive level. Functional play consists of simple, repetitive physical actions performed for the sheer pleasure and mastery of doing them, such as an infant repeatedly shaking a rattle, banging objects together, splashing water, or putting things in and out of a container. These actions map directly onto the sensorimotor infant's way of understanding the world through movement and sensation, hence the name. As the child matures cognitively, play develops through further stages: symbolic or pretend play emerges in the preoperational period (roughly ages 2 to 7), when the child can let one thing represent another, using a banana as a telephone or feeding a doll; constructive play involves purposefully building and creating; and games with rules appear in middle childhood once the child can grasp and follow shared, cooperative rules, reflecting more advanced social and logical thinking. Because the question asks for the first stage emerging in infancy, and functional practice play corresponds to the sensorimotor infant, that is the correct answer.", "traps": {"a": "Symbolic (pretend) play, using one object to stand for another, emerges later in the preoperational stage, not in earliest infancy.", "c": "Games with rules appear in middle childhood and require understanding of shared, agreed-upon rules, making them the most advanced stage, not the first.", "d": "Constructive play, deliberately building or creating things, develops after functional play once the child manipulates objects with a goal in mind."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes \"behavioural activation\" in psychotherapy?", "concept": "Behavioural activation is a structured, present-focused treatment for depression that grew out of the behavioural tradition. Its logic traces to Charles Ferster and later Peter Lewinsohn, who argued that depression is maintained by a loss of response-contingent positive reinforcement: when someone feels low they withdraw, do less, and contact fewer sources of reward, which deepens the low mood in a vicious cycle. Neil Jacobson and colleagues then isolated activation as a powerful stand-alone treatment, showing in a component analysis that it worked as well as full cognitive therapy for many patients. The core method is to help the client gradually re-engage in meaningful, valued and pleasurable activities through activity monitoring, scheduling and graded task assignment, acting from the outside in rather than waiting to feel motivated first. By increasing contact with natural reinforcers, mood lifts and avoidance shrinks. The distractors describe other approaches: exploring early childhood is psychodynamic, relaxation training is a behavioural technique aimed at anxiety, and hypnosis is a separate modality entirely. The defining feature of behavioural activation is deliberately scheduling rewarding action to break the withdrawal-depression loop. A helpful anchor: it treats depression by changing what you do before changing how you feel, so the phrase 'engage in meaningful activities to counteract depression' captures its essence precisely.", "traps": {"b": "Analysing early childhood experiences is psychodynamic work, the opposite of behavioural activation's present-focused, action-oriented approach.", "c": "Relaxation training targets anxiety physiology, not the activity-depression cycle that behavioural activation addresses.", "d": "Hypnosis for symptom relief is a distinct technique unrelated to scheduling rewarding behaviour."}}, {"q": "According to DSM-5, which of the following is required for a diagnosis of Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)?", "concept": "Autism was first described by Leo Kanner (1943), and a verbally fluent picture by Hans Asperger (1944). DSM-IV used a triad (social interaction, communication, restricted behaviour) and separate diagnoses such as autistic disorder and Asperger's disorder. DSM-5 (2013) merged these into one autism spectrum disorder with two criterion domains. Criterion A: persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction across multiple contexts, shown in all three of social-emotional reciprocity (for example, failure of back-and-forth conversation), nonverbal communicative behaviours (poor eye contact, few gestures), and developing, maintaining and understanding relationships. Criterion B: restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, at least two of four: stereotyped movements, speech or object use (echolalia, lining up toys); insistence on sameness; highly restricted, fixated interests; and hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input. Symptoms must be present in the early developmental period (C), cause clinically significant impairment (D), and not be better explained by intellectual disability or global developmental delay (E). Language and intellectual impairment are now specifiers ('with or without accompanying language impairment', 'with or without accompanying intellectual impairment'), and severity is rated Level 1 to 3 by the support needed. Example: Kabir, aged four, speaks in full sentences but repeats film dialogue, avoids peers and screams when his route to school changes; he meets criteria despite normal speech. Neighbour: social (pragmatic) communication disorder has Criterion A type deficits without Criterion B. Option b is right because persistent social communication and interaction deficits across contexts form Criterion A, which every ASD diagnosis requires, together with Criterion B.", "traps": {"a": "Delayed speech was one listed symptom of DSM-IV autistic disorder (in its communication domain), but DSM-5 removed it from the criteria. Many autistic people speak on time or fluently, as in Asperger's cases, while many late talkers have a language disorder or hearing loss and no autism. Delay is now recorded with the specifier 'with or without accompanying language impairment'. It tempts because late speech is often what parents notice first.", "c": "Intellectual disability (intellectual developmental disorder) means deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning with onset in the developmental period. It co-occurs with autism in a sizeable share of cases, which DSM-5 records with the specifier 'with or without accompanying intellectual impairment', but it is neither required nor sufficient. Criterion E even states the social deficits must not be better explained by intellectual disability. It tempts because of the frequent overlap.", "d": "Hyperactivity and impulsivity form one of the two symptom domains of attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), which needs six or more symptoms (five from age 17) for at least 6 months, with several present before age 12 and in two or more settings. Since DSM-5, ADHD and ASD can be diagnosed together, and restless behaviour is common in autistic children. It tempts for that reason, but hyperactivity is not an ASD criterion."}}, {"q": "Uniting various qualitative methods with quantitative methods can be called as:", "concept": "Combining qualitative and quantitative methods is called triangulation, so the answer is b. Triangulation is the strategy of using more than one method, data source, theory, or investigator to study the same phenomenon, so that the strengths of one approach compensate for the weaknesses of another and convergent findings increase confidence in the conclusions. The term borrows from navigation and surveying, where a position is fixed by taking bearings from multiple points. Norman Denzin distinguished several types: methodological triangulation (mixing methods such as qualitative interviews with quantitative surveys, which is exactly what the question describes), data triangulation (different times, places, or samples), investigator triangulation (multiple researchers), and theory triangulation (multiple theoretical lenses). The rationale is that quantitative methods offer breadth, measurement, and generalisability while qualitative methods offer depth, meaning, and context, so uniting them yields a richer and more trustworthy picture and guards against the bias inherent in any single method. This is the conceptual core of mixed methods research. The discriminating cue in the item is uniting various qualitative methods with quantitative methods, which is the textbook description of methodological triangulation. The exam point is to attach the navigation derived word triangulation to the idea of cross checking findings through multiple complementary approaches.", "traps": {"a": "Coalition denotes an alliance of parties or groups, not a methodological strategy of combining research approaches.", "c": "Multidimensional approach is vague and not the established term; triangulation is the specific methodological label for mixing qualitative and quantitative methods.", "d": "Co operative inquiry is a particular participatory research method (Heron and Reason) in which people research with rather than on each other, not the general act of combining methods."}}, {"q": "The researcher who formulated the theory which explains how we form artificial concepts rather than natural concepts by developing a hypothesis is:", "concept": "The researcher who explained how we form artificial rather than natural concepts by developing and testing hypotheses is Jerome Bruner, so the answer is c. Concepts are mental categories that group objects, events, or ideas by shared features. Cognitive psychology distinguishes natural concepts, which are fuzzy, real world categories like bird or furniture that we learn through everyday experience and that are organised around prototypes (a line associated with Eleanor Rosch), from artificial concepts, which are precisely defined categories created for study, learned by figuring out the rule that defines membership. Jerome Bruner, in his classic work A Study of Thinking (with Goodnow and Austin), investigated how people learn artificial concepts in the laboratory and described concept attainment as an active process of hypothesis testing. The learner forms a hypothesis about which features define the concept, tests it against instances classified as examples or non examples, and revises the hypothesis based on feedback, using systematic strategies such as conservative focusing. This portrayed the concept learner as an active, strategic problem solver developing and checking hypotheses, in contrast to passive association. The exam discriminator is the pairing of artificial concept formation through hypothesis development and testing with Bruner, distinguishing his work from Rosch's prototype based account of natural concepts and from Hull and Watson's behaviourist frameworks.", "traps": {"a": "Clark Hull is a behaviourist known for drive reduction theory and mathematical learning laws, not the hypothesis testing theory of artificial concept formation.", "b": "Eleanor Rosch is known for prototype theory of natural concepts and basic level categories, the contrasting account to Bruner's artificial concept work.", "d": "John Watson founded behaviourism (stimulus response conditioning) and did not develop a hypothesis testing theory of concept attainment."}}, {"q": "Which of the following psychologists is associated with the development of the first intelligence test?", "concept": "The first practical intelligence test was developed by the French psychologist Alfred Binet, together with his collaborator Theodore Simon. In 1904 the French government commissioned Binet to devise a way to identify schoolchildren who needed special educational assistance, and the resulting Binet-Simon Scale (1905) measured children's performance on tasks of judgment, comprehension, and reasoning. Its 1908 revision grouped the tasks by age and introduced the mental level (later called mental age), the age level at which a child was performing. This scale was the foundation of modern intelligence testing. It was later revised by Lewis Terman at Stanford University into the Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, which adopted the intelligence quotient (IQ), a term from William Stern, computed as mental age divided by chronological age times 100. The distractors are unrelated to this achievement: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, Jean Piaget studied stages of cognitive development (though he briefly worked in Binet's laboratory), and B. F. Skinner pioneered operant conditioning. Because Alfred Binet developed the first intelligence test, option c is correct.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis and had nothing to do with intelligence testing.", "b": "Jean Piaget studied cognitive development in children, not the creation of the first intelligence test (though he briefly worked in Binet's lab).", "d": "B. F. Skinner was a behaviorist focused on operant conditioning, not intelligence testing."}}, {"q": "Who reformulated the \"frustration-aggression hypothesis\"?", "concept": "The correct answer is Berkowitz. The original frustration-aggression hypothesis was put forward in 1939 by a Yale group, John Dollard, Neal Miller, Leonard Doob, Orval Mowrer, and Robert Sears, who proposed a strong and direct link: frustration (blocking of goal-directed behavior) always leads to some form of aggression, and aggression is always the result of frustration. This absolute formulation proved too rigid, since frustration does not always produce aggression and aggression can arise without frustration. Leonard Berkowitz reformulated the hypothesis in a more nuanced, cognitive-associationist way. He argued that frustration produces a readiness for aggression by generating negative affect (anger), but whether aggression actually occurs depends on the presence of situational cues, particularly aggressive cues in the environment. His famous cue-arousal or weapons-effect research showed that the mere presence of a weapon could increase aggressive responses. Berkowitz thus shifted the model from an inevitable frustration-to-aggression chain to one in which negative emotion plus aggression-eliciting cues jointly determine aggressive behavior, integrating cognition and environmental context. Because the significant reformulation of the frustration-aggression hypothesis is credited to Leonard Berkowitz, option c is correct.", "traps": {"a": "John Dollard was among the original proposers of the frustration-aggression hypothesis, not its reformulator.", "b": "Neal Miller was a co-author of the original hypothesis and later softened it, but the major reformulation is credited to Berkowitz.", "d": "Richard Lazarus is known for his cognitive appraisal theory of stress and emotion, not for this reformulation."}}, {"q": "The concept of the “collective unconscious” was given by:", "concept": "The correct answer is Carl Jung. The collective unconscious is the cornerstone of Jung's analytical psychology and the feature that most sharply distinguishes him from Freud. Whereas Freud's unconscious was personal, built from an individual's own repressed experiences, Jung proposed a deeper layer of the psyche shared by all human beings, inherited rather than acquired. This collective unconscious is populated by archetypes: universal, innate templates or predispositions that shape how we experience the world, such as the Mother, the Hero, the Shadow (the disowned dark side of the self), the Anima and Animus (the feminine within men and masculine within women), and the Self (the archetype of wholeness and integration). Jung drew evidence from the recurrence of similar myths, symbols, and motifs across unrelated cultures and from the universal imagery appearing in dreams. The collective unconscious does not contain specific memories but rather inherited potentials for organising experience. This concept underpins Jungian therapy, where the goal of individuation is to integrate conscious and unconscious material, including archetypal contents, into a balanced whole. Understanding it is essential for distinguishing the three major depth-psychology systems of Freud, Adler, and Jung.", "traps": {"a": "Sigmund Freud described the personal unconscious built from individual repression, not a shared inherited one; this distinction is precisely what separates him from Jung.", "b": "Alfred Adler emphasised inferiority feelings and striving for superiority, not a collective unconscious.", "d": "Karen Horney focused on basic anxiety and neurotic needs arising from disturbed social relationships, a culturally oriented neo-Freudian view."}}, {"q": "Adreno-cortico-tropic hormone is released by", "concept": "The stress response runs along the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, and knowing which gland does what is essential. The hypothalamus first releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH). CRH travels a short portal route to the anterior pituitary (the adenohypophysis), which responds by secreting adrenocorticotropic hormone, ACTH. ACTH then enters the bloodstream and acts on the adrenal cortex, stimulating it to produce glucocorticoids such as cortisol. Cortisol mobilizes energy and, through negative feedback, dampens further CRH and ACTH release. So the anterior pituitary is the source of ACTH; the adrenal gland is merely its downstream target. It helps to separate the two lobes of the pituitary: the anterior lobe is true glandular tissue that manufactures its own hormones (ACTH, growth hormone, TSH, FSH, LH, prolactin), whereas the posterior lobe is neural tissue that only stores and releases hormones made in the hypothalamus (oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone). Mixing up 'the gland that releases the hormone' with 'the gland the hormone acts on' is the trap here, and the HPA cascade makes the direction unmistakable: hypothalamus to anterior pituitary to adrenal cortex. Therefore ACTH is released by the anterior pituitary gland.", "traps": {"a": "The adrenal gland is the target of ACTH, releasing cortisol in response, not the source of ACTH itself.", "c": "The posterior pituitary stores and releases oxytocin and vasopressin, not ACTH.", "d": "Sweat glands are exocrine structures of thermoregulation and have no role in secreting ACTH."}}, {"q": "This theorist argued that newborn creatures imprint on their caregivers during a critical period in early development.", "concept": "The theorist is Konrad Lorenz, the Austrian ethologist who demonstrated and named imprinting. Imprinting is a rapid, largely irreversible form of early learning in which a young animal forms an attachment to and follows the first moving object it encounters, normally its mother, during a fixed and brief critical period shortly after birth or hatching. Lorenz famously showed this with greylag goslings: hatchlings exposed to him rather than to their mother during the sensitive window imprinted on him and followed him as if he were their parent, even preferring him to their own species. Imprinting illustrates several ethological principles: behaviour can be biologically prepared and triggered by specific stimuli, learning can be time locked to a critical period, and the function is adaptive because following the mother keeps the vulnerable young protected and fed. Lorenz's work, alongside Niko Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch, earned a Nobel Prize and laid groundwork that John Bowlby drew on for attachment theory in humans. The exam discriminator is the combination of newborn creatures, imprint, caregivers, and critical period, all of which are Lorenz's signature terms. Bowlby and Ainsworth applied attachment ideas to human infants, but the specific phenomenon of imprinting during a critical period in young animals is Lorenz's contribution.", "traps": {"a": "Mary Ainsworth devised the Strange Situation and classified human attachment styles; she did not study imprinting in newborn animals.", "b": "John Bowlby built human attachment theory and borrowed from ethology, but imprinting itself was demonstrated and named by Lorenz, not Bowlby.", "c": "Erik Erikson described psychosocial stages of human development, an entirely different framework with no concept of animal imprinting."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is not a treatment developed from classical conditioning principles?", "concept": "The correct answer is token economies (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968). It is important to distinguish therapies derived from classical (Pavlovian) conditioning, described by Ivan Pavlov, from those derived from operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner. Classical conditioning concerns associations between stimuli and reflexive responses, and several exposure-based and aversive therapies flow from it. Flooding exposes the client, all at once and at full intensity, to the feared stimulus without escape, so that the conditioned fear response extinguishes when no harm follows. Systematic desensitization, developed by Joseph Wolpe, pairs graded exposure to the feared stimulus with relaxation (counterconditioning via reciprocal inhibition). Aversion therapy pairs an undesirable behaviour or stimulus (such as alcohol) with an unpleasant unconditioned stimulus (such as a nausea-inducing drug) so that the behaviour comes to elicit aversion. All three rest on stimulus-response associations, that is, classical conditioning. Token economies, by contrast, are based on operant conditioning: desired behaviours are reinforced with tokens (secondary reinforcers) that can later be exchanged for backup rewards, shaping voluntary behaviour through its consequences. Because token economies work through operant reinforcement rather than classical stimulus-stimulus association, they are the technique not developed from classical conditioning principles, making option b correct.", "traps": {"a": "Flooding exposes the client to the feared stimulus to extinguish a conditioned fear, rooted in classical conditioning.", "c": "Systematic desensitisation is a classic counterconditioning technique based on classical conditioning.", "d": "Aversion therapy pairs an unwanted behaviour with an aversive stimulus, a classical conditioning procedure."}}, {"q": "Substance use disorder has the following patterns EXCEPT:", "concept": "The correct answer is drug abuse, because it is the one term in the list that is not a recognized diagnostic pattern within the ICD framework for substance use disorders; the others (acute intoxication and withdrawal, dependence syndrome, and harmful use) are formal ICD-10 categories. The question reflects ICD-10's classification of mental and behavioral disorders due to psychoactive substance use, which organizes substance-related conditions into specific clinical syndromes. Acute intoxication is the transient disturbance of consciousness, cognition, perception, affect, or behavior directly following substance use; withdrawal state is the cluster of symptoms occurring on reducing or stopping a substance after repeated heavy use. Dependence syndrome is the core construct, defined by a cluster of features including a strong desire or compulsion to take the substance, impaired control over use, a withdrawal state, tolerance, neglect of alternative interests, and persistence despite harm. Harmful use is the ICD-10 pattern of substance use that is actually causing damage to physical or mental health, without yet meeting full dependence criteria. The term drug abuse, by contrast, belongs to the older DSM tradition (DSM-IV used substance abuse versus dependence) and is not an ICD diagnostic category, which is why it is the exception. The concept to master is the ICD-10 substance-use scheme and how it differs from DSM terminology, since Indian clinical training and RCI-regulated exams predominantly follow ICD.", "traps": {"a": "Acute intoxication and withdrawal are both legitimate ICD-10 diagnostic patterns for substance-related disorders, so this option does not represent the exception.", "b": "Dependence syndrome is the central ICD-10 category for substance use disorder, defined by compulsion, impaired control, tolerance, and withdrawal, so it is a recognized pattern and not the exception.", "c": "Harmful use is a formal ICD-10 category denoting use that causes actual physical or psychological damage, so it is a valid pattern rather than the term that does not belong."}}, {"q": "Which of the following best reflects a limitation of correlational research designs?", "concept": "Correlational research measures the degree of relationship between two or more variables as they naturally occur, without manipulating them. Its central limitation is the maxim that correlation does not imply causation. Two problems drive this. First is the directionality problem: if variables A and B are related, the design cannot tell whether A causes B, B causes A, or the influence is mutual. Second is the third-variable problem: an unmeasured confound may cause both A and B, producing a spurious association. For instance, a correlation between ice-cream sales and drowning is driven by a third variable, hot weather, not by either causing the other. Only a true experiment, with manipulation of an independent variable and random assignment to conditions, can support causal claims. Option a is false because correlational designs cannot establish causality; option c is false because random assignment is a feature of experiments; option d is false because correlational methods are ubiquitous. Option b correctly names the inability to determine the direction of influence, which is a genuine and defining limitation.", "traps": {"a": "This is stated as a strength and it is false: correlation cannot establish causality, so it is not a limitation as worded.", "c": "Random assignment belongs to experiments, not correlational designs, so this statement is simply wrong.", "d": "Correlational designs are extremely common in psychology, so this is factually incorrect."}}, {"q": "Who introduced the concept of \"flow\" in psychology?", "concept": "The correct answer is Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi. Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi introduced the concept of flow, the mental state of complete absorption in an activity in which a person is fully immersed, energised, and focused, losing track of time and self-consciousness. Flow occurs when the challenge of a task is well matched to the person's skill level: too easy and one is bored, too hard and one is anxious, but in the balanced sweet spot, engagement becomes optimal and intrinsically rewarding. Csikszentmihalyi is a founding figure of positive psychology, and his work on flow is central to understanding intrinsic motivation, creativity, and well-being, so option C is correct. Carl Rogers (option A), a humanistic psychologist, developed client-centred (person-centred) therapy and the idea of unconditional positive regard and the fully functioning person, not flow. Abraham Maslow (option B) is known for the hierarchy of needs and for the concept of peak experiences and self-actualisation; peak experiences resemble flow in being deeply absorbing, but they are a distinct idea, and Maslow did not coin flow. Viktor Frankl (option D), a survivor of the Holocaust, founded logotherapy and emphasised the human search for meaning as the primary motivational force. The concept of flow remains a cornerstone of research on optimal experience.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Rogers developed client-centred therapy and the concept of the fully functioning person, not flow.", "b": "Abraham Maslow created the hierarchy of needs and studied peak experiences, which resemble but are not the concept of flow.", "d": "Viktor Frankl founded logotherapy and wrote about meaning in life, not flow."}}, {"q": "Identify the correct option for Projective tests of Personality: i) Rorschach & TAT, ii) Eysenck Personality Questionnaire, iii) 16 PF, iv) MCMI-III.", "concept": "Only Rorschach and TAT are projective tests, so 'Only i' is correct. The crucial distinction is between projective and objective (self-report) personality tests. Projective tests present deliberately ambiguous, unstructured stimuli and ask the person to interpret them, on the assumption (the projective hypothesis) that people project their unconscious needs, conflicts, and personality onto the vague material because there is no obviously right answer to constrain them. The Rorschach Inkblot Test (ten symmetrical inkblots, devised by Hermann Rorschach in 1921) and the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT, ambiguous scene cards about which the person tells a story, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan) are the two classic projective instruments. By contrast, objective tests use structured items with fixed response formats (true/false or rating scales) that are scored against norms. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire (measuring extraversion, neuroticism, and psychoticism), Cattell's 16 Personality Factor questionnaire (the 16 PF), and the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory (MCMI-III) are all objective self-report inventories, not projective. So among the four listed, only item (i) names projective tests, making 'Only i' the right answer.", "traps": {"a": "'Both i and ii' is wrong because item (ii), the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire, is an objective self-report inventory, not a projective test.", "c": "'i, ii and iv' wrongly includes the Eysenck questionnaire and the MCMI-III, both of which are structured objective inventories rather than projective techniques.", "d": "'None' is wrong because item (i), Rorschach and TAT, genuinely are projective tests, so at least one option qualifies."}}, {"q": "Which social psychological theory suggests that individuals perceive others in terms of their positive or negative traits, leading to a \"halo\" effect?", "concept": "The answer is a: implicit personality theory is the set of unspoken beliefs each of us holds about which traits go together, and it is what produces the halo effect. Jerome Bruner and Renato Tagiuri (1954) gave the idea its name. When we meet someone we know only a few of their traits, so we fill in the rest from our private 'theory' of personality: warm people must also be generous, clever people must also be hard-working, attractive people must also be kind. The halo effect is the best-known product of this filling-in. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after finding that army officers who rated a soldier high on physique also rated him high on intelligence, loyalty and leadership; one good impression spread to unrelated traits. It also works in reverse, when one bad trait darkens everything (the 'horns' effect). For example, Kavya's new colleague Rohan is cheerful and well dressed, so she assumes he is also competent and honest, though she has no evidence on either. Halo effects bias interviews, teachers' grading and performance appraisals, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. Option a is right because only implicit personality theory explains why a single positive or negative trait colours our judgements of all the others. Exam trap: the primacy effect (Solomon Asch, 1946) also shapes first impressions, but it is about the order of information (early traits weigh most), not about one trait spreading to the rest.", "traps": {"b": "Self-perception theory is Daryl Bem's (1967, 1972) idea that we infer our own attitudes by watching our own behaviour, as an outside observer would. It is about knowing ourselves, not about one trait of another person colouring the rest.", "c": "Trait-ascription bias is the tendency to see our own behaviour as flexible and driven by the situation while seeing other people's behaviour as fixed, predictable traits. It contrasts self and others; it does not explain the spread of one trait to unrelated ones.", "d": "The primacy effect, shown by Solomon Asch (1946) with the list 'intelligent, industrious, impulsive, critical, stubborn, envious', is the greater weight given to information that comes first. It explains order effects in impressions, not the halo."}}, {"q": "The term 'Inferiority Complex' was coined by:", "concept": "The answer is Alfred Adler because the term inferiority complex was coined by Adler, the founder of Individual Psychology and an early associate of Freud who broke away to develop his own theory emphasizing social and motivational factors over sexual drives. Adler proposed that all human beings begin life with normal feelings of inferiority, because children are small, dependent, and surrounded by more capable adults. These feelings act as a healthy motivating force, driving people to strive for competence, mastery, and what he called superiority, meaning growth and self-improvement rather than dominance over others. When this normal striving goes wrong, two problems can arise. An inferiority complex is an exaggerated, persistent sense of inadequacy and helplessness in which the person feels overwhelmed and unable to compensate, often stemming from organ inferiority, pampering, or neglect in childhood. Its counterpart, the superiority complex, is an overcompensating facade of boastfulness or arrogance that masks the underlying inferiority. Central to Adler's system are also concepts like striving for superiority, the style of life, social interest, and birth order. Recognizing inferiority complex as distinctly Adlerian helps separate him from Freud, who emphasized the unconscious and psychosexual stages, and from Jung, who developed the collective unconscious and archetypes. So the coining of inferiority complex belongs to Alfred Adler.", "traps": {"a": "Franz Alexander was a psychoanalyst known for foundational work in psychosomatic medicine and the corrective emotional experience, not for the concept of the inferiority complex.", "c": "Gordon Allport was a trait theorist of personality, known for cardinal, central, and secondary traits and for functional autonomy, not for Adlerian concepts.", "d": "Carl Jung founded analytical psychology with concepts like the collective unconscious, archetypes, and introversion-extraversion, but the inferiority complex is Adler's term, not Jung's."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is an inhibitory neurotransmitter?", "concept": "Neurotransmitters are broadly grouped by their effect on the receiving neuron's membrane. An excitatory transmitter depolarizes the postsynaptic cell, making it more likely to fire; an inhibitory transmitter hyperpolarizes it, making firing less likely. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the principal inhibitory neurotransmitter of the mammalian central nervous system. It works mainly through GABA-A receptors, which are chloride ion channels: when GABA binds, chloride flows into the neuron, the inside becomes more negative, and the cell is pushed away from its firing threshold. This calming, dampening action is why GABA underlies the sedative and anxiolytic effects of drugs that enhance it, such as benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and alcohol, which potentiate GABA-A signalling. Its counterpart, glutamate, is the main excitatory transmitter, and the balance between the two keeps neural activity stable; too little GABAergic inhibition contributes to anxiety and seizures. The other options are catecholamines (norepinephrine, dopamine, epinephrine) that are predominantly excitatory or modulatory and drive arousal, reward, and stress. For exam purposes, remember the pairing: GABA equals inhibition, glutamate equals excitation. That makes GABA the correct choice for an inhibitory neurotransmitter.", "traps": {"a": "Norepinephrine is largely an excitatory catecholamine driving arousal and the sympathetic fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) response.", "c": "Dopamine is a modulatory catecholamine central to reward and movement, not classified as the brain's chief inhibitory transmitter.", "d": "Epinephrine (adrenaline) is an excitatory catecholamine and stress hormone, not an inhibitory transmitter."}}, {"q": "A tribe of Native Americans have a specific model of parenting that has been practised for generations. Mothers respond quickly to infant distress, which leads to decreased episodes of crying and tantrums. They also believe in the power of touch, and hold their children almost constantly through the first three years of the baby's life, which also leads to decreased distress. Generations of the tribe's children are quiet, even tempered, obedient, and respectful. The behaviour of the tribe's children is best described by which of the following terms?", "concept": "The correct answer is learnt behaviour. Learnt (learned) behaviour is behaviour acquired through experience and environmental influence rather than inherited genetically. In this scenario, the calm, even-tempered, obedient behaviour of the tribe's children arises directly from consistent parenting practices: prompt responsiveness to distress and near-constant physical contact during the crucial first three years. Because the behaviour is shaped by nurture, socialisation, and repeated experience rather than being present at birth, it is learned rather than innate. This connects to attachment theory by John Bowlby and Mary Ainsworth, who argued that sensitive, responsive caregiving fosters secure attachment and emotional regulation, and to social learning theory by Albert Bandura, who showed that children acquire behaviour patterns through modelling and reinforcement in their environment. The key contrast is nature versus nurture: innate behaviour (option B) is genetically pre-programmed and universal, whereas the described temperament is transmitted culturally across generations through childrearing methods. Covert behaviour concerns hidden internal states, and complex behaviour is a vague descriptor that ignores the causal role of environment. The item tests whether the student recognises environmentally acquired behaviour as learned.", "traps": {"a": "Covert behaviour refers to internal, unobservable mental activity (thoughts, feelings); the tribe's children show observable, outward behaviour patterns.", "b": "Innate behaviour is inborn and appears without experience; the calm temperament here is cultivated by consistent parenting practices, so it is acquired, not innate.", "c": "Complex behaviour merely describes behaviour with many components; it does not capture the crucial point that this behaviour is produced by upbringing and environment."}}, {"q": "Systematic desensitisation, in which a client resolves irrational fears through gradual exposure to the fear-producing stimulus, was first developed as a therapy technique by:", "concept": "Systematic desensitisation was developed by Joseph Wolpe in the 1950s, making b correct. Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist working within the behaviour-therapy tradition, built the technique on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: the idea that two incompatible physiological states cannot be experienced at the same time, so if a person is deeply relaxed they cannot simultaneously feel intense anxiety. By repeatedly pairing relaxation with the feared stimulus, the relaxation response comes to inhibit and eventually replace the anxiety response, a form of counterconditioning. The procedure has three core components. First, the therapist trains the client in deep muscle relaxation, often using Jacobson's progressive relaxation. Second, the client and therapist construct an anxiety hierarchy, ranking fear-provoking situations from least to most threatening on a scale of subjective units of distress. Third, while maintaining relaxation, the client is exposed, usually first in imagination and gradually in reality, to each step of the hierarchy, moving up only when the lower item no longer evokes anxiety. Because exposure is graded and paired with relaxation, the client never becomes overwhelmed, distinguishing it from flooding, where the person confronts the most feared stimulus immediately and at full intensity. Systematic desensitisation remains a foundational evidence-based treatment for specific phobias and other anxiety conditions and is one of the clearest applications of classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) principles to therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Carl Rogers founded client-centred (person-centred) therapy, a humanistic approach built on empathy and unconditional positive regard, not a behavioural exposure technique.", "c": "Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis, which treats anxiety through interpretation of unconscious conflict and free association, the opposite of a structured behavioural method.", "d": "Albert Bandura is associated with social learning theory and modelling-based treatments such as participant modelling, not with the relaxation-and-hierarchy method of systematic desensitisation."}}, {"q": "Identify the 'odd one out' from the groups:", "concept": "The correct answer is Borderline personality disorder, which is the odd one out because the other three (paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal) all belong to DSM Cluster A, the odd or eccentric cluster, whereas borderline personality disorder belongs to Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional, and erratic cluster. The DSM organizes the ten personality disorders into three clusters based on descriptive similarity. Cluster A includes paranoid (pervasive distrust and suspiciousness), schizoid (detachment from social relationships and restricted emotional expression), and schizotypal (acute discomfort in relationships plus cognitive-perceptual distortions and eccentric behavior); these share a flavor of social oddness and are thought to share some genetic relationship with the schizophrenia spectrum. Cluster B includes antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic disorders, marked by emotional dysregulation, impulsivity, and interpersonal turmoil. Cluster C (avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive) is the anxious, fearful cluster. Borderline personality disorder specifically is defined by pervasive instability of affect, self-image, and relationships together with marked impulsivity, including fear of abandonment, identity disturbance, self-harm, and chronic emptiness. The concept to master is the cluster structure itself: grouping the disorders by their core thematic resemblance is exactly what lets you spot that three options cluster together as eccentric (Cluster A) while borderline stands apart as dramatic-emotional (Cluster B).", "traps": {"a": "Paranoid personality disorder is a Cluster A (odd/eccentric) disorder marked by pervasive distrust and suspicion of others, so it groups with the other two and is not the outlier.", "b": "Schizoid personality disorder is a Cluster A disorder characterized by detachment and limited emotional expression, again belonging to the eccentric cluster and not standing apart.", "c": "Schizotypal personality disorder is a Cluster A disorder featuring eccentric thinking, perceptual distortions, and social anxiety, so it too fits the odd cluster and is not the odd one out."}}, {"q": "Those sampling methods which clearly specify the likelihood of inclusion of each element in the sample are known as:", "concept": "These are probability sampling methods, so the answer is a. The defining feature of probability sampling is that every element in the population has a known, non zero chance of being selected, and that chance can be specified. Because the selection is governed by chance rather than by the researcher's judgment or convenience, probability sampling supports unbiased estimation, the calculation of sampling error, and statistical generalisation from sample to population. The main probability designs are simple random sampling, where each unit has an equal chance; systematic sampling, selecting every kth unit from a random start; stratified sampling, dividing the population into strata and sampling within each; and cluster sampling, randomly selecting whole groups. The contrast is non probability sampling, including convenience, quota, purposive, and snowball methods, in which the probability of inclusion is unknown and selection rests on accessibility or judgment, so results cannot be generalised with known precision. The exam discriminator is the exact phrase clearly specify the likelihood of inclusion of each element, which is the textbook definition of probability sampling. The deeper point is that knowing the inclusion probabilities is what licenses inferential statistics and estimates of confidence, which is why probability sampling is preferred whenever representative, generalisable conclusions are the goal.", "traps": {"b": "Non probability sampling methods are the opposite case, where the chance of inclusion is unknown and selection is by convenience or judgment, so they cannot specify each element's likelihood.", "c": "Statistical sampling is a loose umbrella phrase, not the precise methodological term for designs defined by known inclusion probabilities.", "d": "Empirical sampling is not a standard category in sampling theory and does not denote the known probability property described."}}, {"q": "Among monocular cues for depth perception is", "concept": "Depth cues split into two families. Binocular cues need both eyes working together, while monocular cues can be picked up with a single eye and are what allow a flat painting or photograph to convey depth. Interposition (also called occlusion) is a monocular cue: when one object partly blocks another, the covered object is judged to be farther away. Other monocular cues studied by perception researchers, and organised into the pictorial cues emphasised by the Gestalt tradition and later by James Gibson, include relative size, linear perspective, texture gradient, aerial perspective, and motion parallax. The two great binocular cues are convergence, the muscular signal from the eyes rotating inward for near objects, and retinal (binocular) disparity, the small difference between the two eyes' images that Charles Wheatstone demonstrated with his stereoscope and that the brain fuses into a vivid sense of solidity. The trap in this item is that convergence and retinal disparity are both real, powerful depth cues, but both are binocular, so neither answers a question asking specifically for a monocular cue. Assimilation is a distractor borrowed from Jean Piaget's developmental theory and has nothing to do with perceiving depth. Interposition is the only listed cue that a one-eyed observer could still exploit.", "traps": {"a": "Convergence is binocular: it relies on the inward rotation of both eyes and the feedback from eye muscles, so it fails with one eye closed.", "b": "Retinal disparity is the classic binocular cue, comparing the two slightly different images the two eyes receive.", "c": "Assimilation is a Piagetian concept about fitting new information into existing schemas, not a depth cue at all."}}, {"q": "A response style or bias, in which there is tendency to agree with test items regardless of their contents is called:", "concept": "The correct answer is the acquiescence effect. Acquiescence (also called the acquiescence response set or yea-saying) is a response bias in which a person tends to agree with, or answer yes or true to, test items regardless of their content, rather than responding to what each item actually asks. Such respondents go along with statements out of habit, agreeableness, deference, or carelessness, which distorts their scores because their answers reflect a general tendency to agree rather than their true position. This threatens the validity of questionnaires and personality inventories. The classic safeguard against acquiescence is to balance the scale with both positively and negatively worded items (reverse-keyed items), so that consistent agreement cannot artificially inflate a score in one direction and genuine respondents must read each item. Acquiescence is one of several response styles or biases that distort self-report measures. Others include social desirability (answering in ways that present oneself favourably rather than truthfully), the central tendency bias (avoiding extreme options and clustering responses in the middle of a rating scale), and the halo effect (letting a global impression colour specific ratings). Because the defining feature here is agreeing with items irrespective of their content, the acquiescence effect is the precise answer.", "traps": {"a": "The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is letting an overall impression of a person bias ratings of their specific separate traits, not blanket agreement with items.", "b": "The central tendency effect is the tendency to avoid extreme ratings and choose middle options, not to agree regardless of content.", "c": "The social desirability effect is answering to look favourable to others, which is content-sensitive, unlike indiscriminate agreement."}}, {"q": "The physical attractiveness of a person, or any other trait, can influence multiple judgements and lead to a more favourable view of the whole personality. This tendency is called:", "concept": "The answer is c: when one attractive trait colours our judgement of the whole person, it is called the halo effect. Edward Thorndike (1920) named it after studying how army officers rated their soldiers on physique, intelligence, leadership and character. The ratings correlated far too highly: a soldier judged handsome and well built was also rated more intelligent and more loyal, as if one good impression cast a glow over everything else. The halo is a general impression, or one striking trait, spreading to other, unrelated traits. Physical attractiveness is the strongest trigger. Karen Dion, Ellen Berscheid and Elaine Walster (1972) showed students photographs of attractive, average and unattractive people; the attractive ones were judged kinder, more sociable, more successful and happier: 'what is beautiful is good'. The reverse, one bad trait dragging down the whole judgement, is sometimes called the horns effect. The halo also biases ratings by teachers, interviewers and clinicians, which is why good rating scales define each trait separately. At a job interview, for example, Rohan is smart, confident and well dressed, and the panel also assumes he is honest and hard-working, although they have no evidence on those traits. Option c is right because the stem describes one trait shaping many unrelated judgements and a more favourable view of the whole personality. Exam trap: the Pygmalion effect also involves expectations about a person, but it concerns how those expectations change the person's actual performance; the halo effect is a bias in the perceiver's judgement only.", "traps": {"a": "The primacy effect, shown by Solomon Asch (1946) with trait lists read in different orders, is the greater weight given to information that comes first; it concerns the order of information, not one trait spreading to others.", "b": "The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is a self-fulfilling prophecy in which teachers' raised expectations led to real gains in pupils' performance; it changes the target's behaviour rather than biasing the observer's overall view.", "d": "Non-common effects belong to Edward Jones and Keith Davis's correspondent inference theory (1965): we infer a person's intentions from the unique outcomes of the option they chose; it is an attribution rule, not a rating bias."}}, {"q": "Which of the following defense mechanism is seen in patients with borderline personality disorder?", "concept": "The correct answer is splitting. Splitting is the central, characteristic defence mechanism of borderline personality disorder (BPD). Splitting is the inability to integrate the positive and negative qualities of oneself or others into a coherent, balanced whole, so that people (including the self) are experienced as either all good or all bad, with no middle ground. A person using splitting may idealise someone one moment (seeing them as perfect, wonderful, the only one who understands) and then, after a minor disappointment, abruptly devalue the same person (seeing them as cruel, worthless, an enemy). This produces the intense, unstable, and stormy interpersonal relationships that are a core feature of BPD, along with an unstable sense of self, emotional dysregulation, fear of abandonment, and impulsivity. Splitting is rooted in Melanie Klein's object-relations theory and Otto Kernberg's account of borderline personality organisation, where the failure to integrate good and bad object-representations is seen as the underlying structural problem. The all-good versus all-bad oscillation directly explains the dramatic swings in how borderline patients view their relationships and themselves. Because splitting is the signature defence of BPD, it is the correct answer, and it is a frequent clinical and exam association.", "traps": {"b": "Regression (reverting to earlier developmental behaviour under stress) can occur in many conditions but is not the defining defence of borderline personality disorder.", "c": "Undoing (performing acts to magically cancel an unacceptable impulse) is characteristic of obsessive-compulsive disorder, not BPD.", "d": "Sublimation (channelling impulses into socially valued activity) is a mature, healthy defence, not the primitive splitting seen in BPD."}}, {"q": "Parts of the autonomic nervous system are:", "concept": "The answer is sympathetic and parasympathetic because these are the two divisions of the autonomic nervous system. To get this right you must hold the full architecture of the nervous system in mind. The nervous system splits first into the central nervous system (brain and spinal cord) and the peripheral nervous system (all the nerves outside it). The peripheral nervous system then divides into the somatic nervous system, which carries sensory information in and controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement, and the autonomic nervous system, which controls involuntary functions of internal organs, glands, and smooth muscle (heart rate, digestion, pupil size, sweating). The autonomic nervous system in turn has two branches that generally oppose and balance each other. The sympathetic division mobilizes the body for fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon): it increases heart rate, dilates pupils, redirects blood to muscles, and releases stored energy during stress or emergency. The parasympathetic division promotes rest-and-digest: it conserves energy, slows the heart, and supports digestion and recovery once the threat passes. So the autonomic nervous system equals sympathetic plus parasympathetic. The trap options describe other valid nervous-system distinctions but at the wrong level: central versus peripheral is the top-level split of the whole nervous system, and voluntary versus involuntary describes the somatic versus autonomic distinction, not the parts within the autonomic system itself. Keep the hierarchy clean and the two divisions, sympathetic and parasympathetic, follow immediately.", "traps": {"b": "Central and peripheral is the highest-level division of the entire nervous system, not the divisions WITHIN the autonomic nervous system.", "c": "Voluntary and involuntary distinguishes the somatic from the autonomic system; it is not the two branches of the autonomic system itself.", "d": "Cerebrospinal and cortical are not divisions of the autonomic nervous system at all; cerebrospinal refers to fluid or spinal nerves and cortical refers to the cerebral cortex."}}, {"q": "Principle of identity in early childhood refers to", "concept": "In Jean Piaget's account of cognitive development, summarised by Jean Piaget and Barbel Inhelder in The Psychology of the Child (English edition 1969), the preoperational child (about 2 to 7 years) gradually grasps identity: the understanding that people and things keep their basic nature even when their form, size or appearance changes. Example: Kabir, aged five, knows the clay is still the same clay after his sister rolls it into a sausage, and that his father in a tiger mask is still his father. Piaget distinguished this qualitative identity (it is the same thing) from conservation, which is quantitative invariance (it is the same amount). A preoperational child may agree that water poured into a tall thin glass is the same water yet insist there is now more of it, because centration fixes attention on the height. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) children justify conservation with three arguments: identity (nothing was added or taken away), reversibility (pour it back and it will be the same) and compensation (taller but thinner). Conservation is mastered unevenly across materials, a pattern Piaget called horizontal decalage: number first, then mass, then weight, then volume. Do not confuse this principle with gender identity, with ego identity in Erik Erikson's (1950) adolescent stage, or with the self-concept. Option b is right because the principle of identity is the recognition that an object's essential characteristics stay fixed even when its appearance changes.", "traps": {"a": "Recognising positive characteristics about oneself describes self-esteem, the evaluative part of the self-concept, measured for example by the Rosenberg Self-Esteem Scale (Morris Rosenberg, 1965). In early childhood self-esteem tends to be high and unrealistic. It tempts because 'identity' in everyday language suggests self-image, but Piaget's principle of identity concerns objects and people staying the same despite surface change, not how positively a child views himself.", "c": "Identifying with one's gender is gender identity. Lawrence Kohlberg's (1966) cognitive-developmental theory describes gender identity (labelling oneself as a boy or girl, around 2 to 3 years), gender stability (knowing gender lasts over time) and gender constancy (knowing it stays the same despite changes in clothing or hairstyle, around 5 to 7). It tempts because gender constancy applies the identity idea to gender, but the option names identification, not invariance.", "d": "Regulating behaviour and attitudes to fit the surrounding world describes self-regulation and socialisation: gradually adjusting conduct to social standards through compliance, delay of gratification and internalised rules. It also echoes Piaget's accommodation, changing one's schemes to fit reality. It tempts because it sounds like a cognitive-developmental achievement of early childhood, but the principle of identity concerns recognising that objects remain the same, not adjusting oneself to others."}}, {"q": "A criticism of the REBT cognitive approach to understanding mental-health problems is that:", "concept": "The correct answer is that it can be direct and confrontational and so might be seen as intimidatory by the client. Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), developed by Albert Ellis, is an active, directive form of cognitive therapy in which the therapist works vigorously to identify and dispute the client's irrational beliefs (such as absolutistic 'musts' and 'shoulds', catastrophising, and self-downing) and to replace them with more rational, flexible beliefs. A recognised criticism of this style is precisely its forcefulness: because Ellis advocated an energetic, sometimes blunt and confrontational approach to challenging irrational thinking, some clients may experience the therapist's disputing as harsh, intimidating, or uncomfortable, and a strong therapeutic alliance can be harder to build, particularly for vulnerable or fragile clients. This contrasts with the gentle, non-directive warmth of Carl Rogers's client-centred therapy. The other options are inaccurate: an over-emphasis on sexuality is a criticism levelled at Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis rather than REBT (option A); REBT and cognitive-behavioural therapy in general have a strong evidence base for treating depression and anxiety, so option C is false; and REBT remains widely practised and influential today, so option D is false. This item tests awareness of the confrontational-style criticism specific to REBT.", "traps": {"a": "Excessive focus on sexuality is a criticism of Freudian psychoanalysis, not of REBT, which centres on irrational beliefs.", "c": "REBT and cognitive-behavioural approaches have substantial evidence of effectiveness for depression, so claiming it has not been shown to help is false.", "d": "REBT remains widely used and relevant, so the claim that it is irrelevant in the 21st century is inaccurate."}}, {"q": "Schizophrenia is most commonly found in :", "concept": "The correct answer is young adults. Schizophrenia is a severe psychotic disorder characterized by positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech), negative symptoms (flat affect, avolition, alogia), and cognitive impairment, described in modern nosology by the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association and historically named by Eugen Bleuler, who coined schizophrenia, building on the dementia praecox concept of Emil Kraepelin. Its typical age of onset falls in late adolescence to early adulthood, most commonly the late teens to mid-twenties for men and roughly the mid-twenties to early thirties for women, with women showing a smaller second peak around midlife. Thus the disorder is most commonly found in young adults. The relatively early onset reflects the neurodevelopmental nature of the illness, in which subtle brain maturation abnormalities interact with the demands and stresses of the transition to adulthood, consistent with the diathesis-stress and dopamine hypotheses of schizophrenia. Onset in childhood or in the elderly does occur but is far less common. For the exam, the key fact is that the peak incidence and most typical presentation cluster in young adulthood.", "traps": {"a": "Onset before the late teens is possible but comparatively uncommon; the peak is early adulthood, not adolescence.", "c": "Middle-age onset is atypical and, when it occurs later in women, is a secondary peak rather than the most common period.", "d": "Late-life onset is rare; schizophrenia characteristically emerges decades earlier."}}, {"q": "Experimental designs with more than one independent variable, in which the different variable levels are combined in a systematic manner, are:", "concept": "Experimental designs with more than one independent variable whose levels are systematically combined are factorial experiments, so the answer is a. A factorial design is one in which two or more independent variables (called factors) are studied simultaneously, and every level of each factor is crossed with every level of the other factors, so that all possible combinations of conditions are tested. For example, a 2 by 3 factorial crosses two levels of one factor with three levels of another, yielding six conditions. The great advantage of factorial designs is efficiency and, crucially, the ability to detect interaction effects, that is, whether the effect of one independent variable depends on the level of another, in addition to the separate main effects of each variable. Interactions are often the most theoretically interesting findings because they reveal that variables do not act in isolation but combine in complex ways. The systematic combination of variable levels mentioned in the question is the defining feature of a factorial design. The exam discriminator is matching more than one independent variable, with levels combined systematically, to factorial experiments, distinguishing them from quasi experiments (which lack random assignment), and from the non standard labels in the other options.", "traps": {"b": "Quasi experiments are defined by the absence of random assignment to conditions, not by having multiple systematically crossed independent variables.", "c": "Systematic experiments is not a recognised design category; the term for crossing multiple factors is factorial.", "d": "Descriptive experiments is not a standard design type; descriptive research is generally non experimental, lacking manipulation of variables."}}, {"q": "Which psychological approach emphasizes learning through stimulus-response associations, mainly via conditioning?", "concept": "Behaviorism is the school of psychology that restricts explanation to observable stimuli and responses, deliberately excluding introspection and unobservable mental events. Its stimulus-response (S-R) framework holds that behavior is acquired through conditioning. Classical conditioning, discovered by Ivan Pavlov, pairs a neutral stimulus with a reflex-eliciting one until the neutral stimulus alone evokes the response. John B. Watson, who wrote the behaviorist manifesto in 1913, applied this to human emotion in the Little Albert study. Operant conditioning, formulated by B. F. Skinner building on the law of effect of Edward Thorndike, explains how consequences (reinforcement and punishment) shape voluntary behavior. All of these treat learning as the formation of associations between stimuli and responses. The cognitive approach (option a) arose partly in reaction to behaviorism, insisting that internal representations mediate behavior; the psychoanalytic (c) and existential (d) approaches address unconscious conflict and meaning respectively. Because option b names the stimulus-response behaviorist approach explicitly, it is correct.", "traps": {"a": "The cognitive approach emphasizes internal mental processes such as attention and memory, explicitly rejecting a pure stimulus-response focus.", "c": "The psychoanalytic approach centers on unconscious drives and childhood conflict, not conditioning.", "d": "The existential approach concerns meaning, freedom, and choice, which is unrelated to stimulus-response learning."}}, {"q": "Summated rating scale is also known as :", "concept": "The correct answer is the Likert scale. A summated rating scale is an attitude-measurement scale in which a respondent indicates their degree of agreement with a set of statements along an ordered response continuum (for example, strongly disagree, disagree, neutral, agree, strongly agree, typically scored 1 to 5), and the responses across all items are added or averaged to yield a total score representing the person's overall attitude, hence summated. This method was devised by Rensis Likert in 1932, so the summated rating scale is synonymous with the Likert scale. Its advantages are ease of construction, reliability, and the ability to measure intensity of feeling. It is important to distinguish it from other classic attitude scales. The Thurstone scale, developed by Louis Leon Thurstone, is the method of equal-appearing intervals, in which judges first assign scale values to statements and respondents endorse those they agree with; option d (equal appearing interval scale) is therefore another name for the Thurstone scale, not the Likert scale. The Guttman scale, developed by Louis Guttman, is a cumulative scale in which agreeing with a stronger item implies agreement with all weaker ones, producing a unidimensional ordering. Because the summated rating method is Likert's, option a is correct.", "traps": {"b": "The Thurstone scale uses judged scale values and is the equal-appearing interval method, not a summated scale.", "c": "The Guttmann scale is a cumulative (unidimensional) scale, distinct from summated rating.", "d": "Equal appearing interval scale is another name for the Thurstone scale, not the summated one."}}, {"q": "Whether or not disadvantaged, people try to change what is perceived as unfair social structure depends on:", "concept": "The answer is c: whether members of a disadvantaged group try to change an unfair social structure depends on the permeability and stability of that structure. Henri Tajfel and John Turner (1979), in social identity theory, argued that people seek a positive social identity from the groups they belong to. When their group compares badly with others, two features of the status system decide how they respond. Permeability is whether group boundaries can be crossed: if an individual can move up into the higher-status group, people choose social mobility, leaving the group as individuals instead of challenging the system. Stability is whether the status order looks fixed or open to change, and it works together with legitimacy (whether the order looks fair): if boundaries are closed and the hierarchy looks unstable and illegitimate, so that a different order can be imagined, people turn to social competition, collectively challenging the dominant group. If the hierarchy looks stable and legitimate, they fall back on social creativity, comparing on a new dimension ('we are poorer but kinder'). Example: Kabir studies at a low-ranked college. If good marks let him transfer to a top college (permeable boundaries), he works for his own transfer; if transfers are closed but the ranking system looks unfair and open to challenge (unstable), his classmates organise together to demand recognition. Option c is right because permeability and stability are exactly the two structural variables the theory names. Exam trap: the distractors borrow dimensions from stereotype research, so they sound scholarly; warmth (sociability) and competence are the two dimensions of the stereotype content model, and morality is a dimension on which groups are judged, not a property of the social structure.", "traps": {"a": "Stability is right, but sociability is not a structural variable: it is part of the warmth dimension in the stereotype content model of Susan Fiske and colleagues (2002), describing how friendly a group seems.", "b": "Permeability is right, but morality is not: it is a dimension on which groups are evaluated (Colin Leach, Naomi Ellemers and Manuela Barreto, 2007), not a feature of the status structure.", "d": "Competence and morality are both dimensions of how groups are perceived and evaluated (competence from the stereotype content model, morality from research on group virtue); neither decides between individual mobility and collective change."}}, {"q": "A person, who believes that he has control over events in his life, has what type of locus of control?", "concept": "Locus of control is a personality construct introduced by Julian Rotter within his social learning theory. It describes the degree to which people believe they, as opposed to external forces, control the outcomes of events in their lives. A person with an internal locus of control believes that outcomes are contingent on their own effort, ability, and decisions, so they tend to take responsibility, persist at tasks, and show greater self-efficacy and achievement motivation. A person with an external locus of control believes that outcomes are governed by luck, fate, chance, or powerful others, and may show more passivity, learned helplessness (Martin Seligman and Steven Maier, 1967), or lower stress-coping when facing difficulties. The construct is measured by Rotter's I-E Scale and relates conceptually to attribution theory and to the self-efficacy work of Albert Bandura. Options c (neutral) and d (situational) are not genuine categories in Rotter's dichotomy. Because believing one has control over one's own life events is the defining feature of an internal locus of control, option b is correct.", "traps": {"a": "An external locus of control is the belief that outcomes are determined by luck, fate, or powerful others, the opposite of an internal locus of control.", "c": "Neutral is not a recognized category in Rotter's locus-of-control construct.", "d": "Situational is not one of the two poles of the locus-of-control dimension."}}, {"q": "The neurotransmitters called feel-good hormones are known as:", "concept": "The correct answer is endorphins. Endorphins (the name is a contraction of 'endogenous morphine') are the body's natural opioid neuropeptides, popularly called the feel-good chemicals because they produce feelings of pleasure, euphoria, and pain relief. They are released by the pituitary gland and the central nervous system in response to stress, pain, exercise, laughter, and certain foods, and they bind to the same opioid receptors that morphine and other opiate drugs act on. By binding to these receptors, endorphins inhibit the transmission of pain signals and trigger a sense of well-being, which is the mechanism behind phenomena such as the runner's high, the analgesia of intense exercise or acupuncture, and stress-induced pain suppression. Beta-endorphin is the most potent of these peptides. Physiologically they form part of the body's intrinsic pain-modulation system, acting in regions such as the periaqueductal grey to dampen ascending pain pathways. While dopamine is also strongly tied to reward and pleasure, the specific phrase feel-good hormones in this question maps to endorphins, given their direct role in producing euphoria and natural pain relief. Understanding endorphins links the psychology of mood and pain to the body's own opioid chemistry.", "traps": {"a": "Dopamine is central to reward, motivation, and pleasure-seeking, but the specific 'feel-good' euphoria-and-pain-relief label in this question maps to endorphins.", "c": "GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter that calms neural activity; it reduces anxiety but is not the euphoria-producing feel-good hormone.", "d": "Acetylcholine is involved in muscle activation, attention, and memory, not in producing pleasurable feel-good states."}}, {"q": "A child's increasing skill at using his muscles is because of:", "concept": "The correct answer is maturation, the genetically programmed, biologically driven sequence of physical and neuromuscular development that unfolds with age relatively independently of specific experience. A child's growing skill at controlling the muscles, such as the orderly progression from sitting to crawling to standing to walking and then to fine motor control, depends primarily on the maturation of the nervous system and musculoskeletal system, especially the myelination of nerve fibers and the development of motor pathways, rather than on practice or teaching. The classic evidence comes from Arnold Gesell, whose co-twin control studies showed that a twin given early training in skills like stair-climbing gained little lasting advantage over the untrained twin once the latter reached the maturational readiness to perform the skill, demonstrating that readiness, not practice, sets the pace. This illustrates the broader developmental concept of the interaction between maturation and learning: maturation provides the biological readiness that makes a skill possible, while learning and experience refine and elaborate it, but the emergence of basic motor competence is maturationally governed. The principle to learn cold is that motor milestones appear in a universal, predictable cephalocaudal (head-to-toe) and proximodistal (center-outward) sequence across cultures and child-rearing practices precisely because they are maturation-driven, which is why pushing or extra exercise cannot meaningfully accelerate a child beyond their maturational readiness.", "traps": {"a": "An opportunity to exercise can refine a skill once the child is biologically ready, but practice alone does not produce the increasing muscular skill; the underlying driver is maturational readiness, as Gesell's twin studies showed.", "b": "Pushing by parents cannot accelerate motor development beyond a child's maturational readiness; encouragement does not override the biological timetable governing neuromuscular growth.", "c": "Learning contributes to elaborating and refining motor skills, but the fundamental emergence of increasing muscular control follows the genetically guided maturation of the nervous and muscular systems, not learning per se."}}, {"q": "In Reality Therapy, developed by William Glasser, which of the following is considered one of the five basic genetic needs?", "concept": "The answer is power or achievement because it is one of the five basic genetic needs in William Glasser's Reality Therapy and its underlying Choice Theory. Glasser, an American psychiatrist, argued that human behaviour is not driven by external circumstances or the past but is chosen in the present as our best attempt to satisfy five fundamental, biologically built-in needs. These five needs are survival (the physiological need for food, shelter, safety, and health), love and belonging (connection, relationships, and being part of a group), power or achievement (a sense of competence, accomplishment, recognition, and self-worth), freedom (autonomy and independence, the ability to make one's own choices), and fun (enjoyment, pleasure, and play). Glasser held that psychological problems typically arise from difficulties in relationships and from unmet needs, and that Reality Therapy helps clients evaluate whether their current choices are effectively meeting these needs, then make more responsible, effective choices in the here and now, taking personal responsibility rather than blaming the past. The distractors are famous concepts from other theorists deliberately placed to test whether you can keep the frameworks separate: self-actualization belongs to Abraham Maslow, the collective unconscious to Carl Jung, and unconditional positive regard to Carl Rogers. Only power or achievement is genuinely one of Glasser's five basic needs, making it the correct answer.", "traps": {"a": "Self-actualization is the pinnacle need in Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, not one of Glasser's five basic genetic needs.", "c": "The collective unconscious is Carl Jung's concept of an inherited shared layer of the unconscious, entirely outside Glasser's Reality Therapy framework.", "d": "Unconditional positive regard is a core condition in Carl Rogers's person-centred therapy, not one of Glasser's basic needs."}}, {"q": "Schizophrenia corresponds to the autistic phase of development and the person fails to develop an adequate and stable symbiosis, was the developmental theory of schizophrenia given by:", "concept": "The developmental theory that schizophrenia corresponds to the autistic phase and a failure to develop stable symbiosis was given by Margaret Mahler, so the answer is c. Mahler was a psychoanalyst who studied the development of the self in infancy through her separation individuation theory. She proposed that the infant moves through phases: an early normal autistic phase (the first weeks, a self absorbed, undifferentiated state with little awareness of the outside world), followed by the normal symbiotic phase (around the second to fifth month, in which the infant experiences a fused, dual unity with the mother as if they were one boundaryless system), and then the separation individuation process, with its subphases of differentiation, practising, rapprochement, and consolidation of individuality. Mahler theorised that severe psychopathology reflects fixation at or failure to negotiate these earliest phases. In her view, the most disturbed conditions correspond to the earliest phases: psychotic disturbance is linked to a failure to move beyond the autistic and symbiotic phases, with schizophrenia reflecting the autistic phase and an inability to form an adequate, stable symbiosis with the mother, leaving the self without a secure foundation. The exam discriminator is the specific Mahlerian language of autistic and symbiotic phases and separation individuation, which distinguishes her account from those of Pao, Arieti, and Jacobson.", "traps": {"a": "Ping Nie Pao wrote on the schizophrenic disorders from an ego psychological view but did not frame it through Mahler's autistic and symbiotic phases.", "b": "Sylvano Arieti authored an influential interpersonal and psychodynamic theory of schizophrenia, but the autistic phase and symbiosis formulation is Mahler's.", "d": "Edith Jacobson contributed to the psychoanalytic understanding of the self and depression, not this specific developmental theory of schizophrenia."}}, {"q": "Which scale of measurement involves categories without any order or ranking, such as car colors?", "concept": "The four levels of measurement were formalized by the psychophysicist Stanley Smith Stevens in 1946, arranged in increasing order of mathematical sophistication: nominal, ordinal, interval, and ratio. A nominal scale is the most basic: it classifies data into distinct categories that have no inherent order, rank, or quantitative relationship. The categories merely name or label groups, such as car colors, gender, blood type, or nationality; numbers, if assigned, are just identifiers and cannot be meaningfully compared as greater or less. An ordinal scale adds rank order (for example, first, second, third, or a Likert agreement rating) but without equal intervals between ranks. An interval scale adds equal, meaningful intervals but has an arbitrary zero (for example, temperature in Celsius). A ratio scale adds a true, absolute zero, permitting statements about ratios (for example, weight, height, or reaction time). Because unordered categories such as car colors are the defining feature of the nominal scale, option a is correct, while the distractors describe higher, ordered levels of measurement.", "traps": {"b": "An ordinal scale has ordered categories (rank), which the question explicitly excludes.", "c": "An interval scale has ordered values with equal intervals but no true zero, more than mere unordered categories.", "d": "A ratio scale has order, equal intervals, and a true zero, far beyond unordered categories."}}, {"q": "Charles Spearman is to Factor Analysis as Wilhelm Wundt is to:", "concept": "The answer is introspection, because the analogy pairs each pioneer with the method he is famous for. Charles Spearman invented factor analysis, the statistical technique of extracting underlying dimensions from correlations, and used it to propose his two-factor theory of intelligence with the general factor g. The analogy therefore asks: what method is Wilhelm Wundt's signature? Wundt founded the first formal psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879, the event conventionally treated as the birth of scientific psychology, and his method was introspection, sometimes called experimental self-observation. Trained observers were exposed to carefully controlled stimuli, such as a metronome beat or a light, and reported the elementary sensations and feelings in their conscious experience. The aim was to analyze consciousness into its basic elements, the program his student Edward Titchener later systematized in America as structuralism. Grasp why the distractors fail by attaching each school to its founder: psychoanalysis belongs to Sigmund Freud, functionalism to William James and the Darwinian question of what consciousness does, and humanism to Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow. Analogy questions like this reward a simple study habit: for every historical figure, memorize one method and one school. Spearman goes with factor analysis and g; Wundt goes with introspection and the Leipzig laboratory; that pairing settles the item instantly.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis is the school and method founded by Sigmund Freud, built on the unconscious and free association, and has no special tie to Wundt.", "c": "Functionalism, associated with William James and influenced by Darwin, studied what mental processes are for rather than their structural contents, and arose largely in opposition to the Wundtian tradition.", "d": "Humanism is the mid-twentieth-century third force of Carl Rogers and Abraham Maslow, emphasizing growth and self-actualization, decades removed from Wundt."}}, {"q": "The phenomenon where a qualified individual is prevented from advancing to higher positions in their career due to an invisible barrier based on gender or minority status is called:", "concept": "The answer is the glass ceiling effect, because an invisible, unacknowledged barrier that prevents qualified individuals, particularly women and minorities, from advancing to higher-level positions despite their qualifications is precisely what the term glass ceiling denotes. Understand the metaphor and the concept behind it. The phrase glass ceiling became popular in the 1980s to describe a barrier that is transparent, meaning it is not written into any official policy and the higher positions are visibly within sight, yet real and impenetrable, so that women and members of minority groups can see the top ranks but cannot reach them because of subtle, systemic, often unconscious discrimination, biases, and organizational cultures that block their promotion. The barrier is invisible because it operates through informal networks, stereotypes about leadership, lack of mentorship, and biased evaluation rather than through explicit rules, which makes it harder to identify and challenge. The concept is central to organizational psychology and the study of gender and workplace inequality, and it has spawned related terms such as the glass cliff (women being promoted to leadership in times of crisis when failure is likely) and the sticky floor (forces keeping women in low-level jobs). Contrast it with the distractors: the Matthew effect is about cumulative advantage, the halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is an impression-formation bias, and the Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is an expectancy-driven self-fulfilling prophecy. The specific idea of an invisible barrier to advancement rooted in gender or minority status is the glass ceiling effect.", "traps": {"a": "The Matthew effect is the phenomenon of accumulated advantage, where those who already have resources or recognition gain even more (the rich get richer), not the invisible barrier blocking advancement based on gender or minority status.", "c": "The halo effect is a cognitive bias in which one positive impression colors judgments of unrelated traits, unrelated to structural barriers to career advancement.", "d": "The Pygmalion effect is the self-fulfilling prophecy in which higher expectations lead to improved performance, an expectancy phenomenon, not a barrier preventing qualified people from rising."}}, {"q": "The Resting Membrane Potential is:", "concept": "The answer is -70 mV to -80 mV because the resting membrane potential of a typical neuron, the steady electrical charge difference across its membrane when it is not firing, is about -70 millivolts (commonly cited in the -65 to -80 mV range), with the inside of the cell negative relative to the outside. This polarized state exists because of an uneven distribution of ions. Inside the neuron there is a high concentration of potassium ions and large negatively charged proteins, while outside there is a high concentration of sodium and chloride ions. Two forces maintain the resting potential: selective membrane permeability and the sodium-potassium pump. At rest the membrane is far more permeable to potassium than to sodium, so potassium leaks out down its concentration gradient, leaving the interior negative. The sodium-potassium pump actively transports three sodium ions out for every two potassium ions in, using ATP, which both maintains the gradients and contributes slightly to the negativity. This resting state is essential because it sets up the potential energy that, when a stimulus opens sodium channels, allows rapid depolarization and the firing of an action potential, which peaks near +30 to +40 mV before repolarizing. So while +40 mV (option b) describes the approximate peak of the action potential, the resting value the question asks for is the negative -70 to -80 mV range.", "traps": {"a": "-40 mV is closer to the threshold of excitation at which an action potential is triggered, not the baseline resting value.", "b": "+40 mV describes the approximate peak voltage at the top of an action potential during depolarization, the opposite phase from rest.", "d": "+70 mV is not a meaningful neuronal value; the resting interior is negative, not strongly positive, so this is simply incorrect."}}, {"q": "Biopsychosocial model of illness was proposed by", "concept": "The biopsychosocial model was proposed by the psychiatrist George L. Engel in a landmark 1977 Science paper. Engel argued that the prevailing biomedical model, which reduced illness to deranged biochemistry and physiology, was too narrow to explain why people become sick, how they experience suffering, and what helps them recover. His model insists that health and illness emerge from the interaction of three levels: biological factors (genetics, pathogens, neurochemistry), psychological factors (beliefs, emotions, coping, behaviour), and social factors (family, culture, socioeconomic status, access to care). Drawing loosely on general systems theory, Engel framed the person as one level in a nested hierarchy running from molecules and cells up to the family, community, and society. The practical upshot is that a good clinician asks not only what is biologically wrong but also how the patient understands the illness and what social circumstances shape its course. This model became the philosophical backbone of health psychology and of modern integrated care. Contrast it with the distractors: Freud's psychoanalysis, Cannon's homeostasis and fight-or-flight physiology, and Lazarus's transactional stress-and-appraisal theory are each important, but none is the tri-level illness framework the question describes. Whenever a stem pairs biological, psychological, and social explicitly, the name to reach for is Engel.", "traps": {"b": "Sigmund Freud built psychoanalysis around intrapsychic drives and the unconscious, not an integrated biological-psychological-social framework for illness.", "c": "Walter Cannon gave us homeostasis and the fight-or-flight response, foundational physiology but not the biopsychosocial model.", "d": "Richard Lazarus is known for the transactional model of stress and cognitive appraisal, a related but distinct contribution."}}, {"q": "The Stroop effect demonstrates interference in", "concept": "The Stroop effect, first reported by John Ridley Stroop in 1935, appears when you are shown a colour word printed in a conflicting ink colour (for example the word RED printed in blue) and are asked to name the ink colour, not read the word. People are reliably slower and more error-prone here than when word and colour match. The reason is that reading is a highly automatic, over-learned process, so the word meaning is processed involuntarily and competes with the deliberate, effortful task of naming the ink. To respond correctly you must suppress the dominant reading response and select the relevant dimension (colour) while ignoring the irrelevant one (meaning). That act of prioritising one source of information and filtering out a competing one is exactly what selective attention does. The interference reflects the cost of resolving this attentional conflict, which is why the Stroop task is a classic measure of attentional control and, in clinical work, of executive dysfunction. It taps the mechanism that lets us focus on goal-relevant input despite distraction, rather than any failure of storage or retrieval. Modern accounts tie it to conflict monitoring by the anterior cingulate cortex, but the core phenomenon it demonstrates is interference within selective attention.", "traps": {"a": "Long-term memory retrieval is not tested by the Stroop task, which measures on-the-spot conflict, not delayed recall of stored information.", "b": "Working memory involves holding and manipulating information, but naming ink colours requires no maintenance of items over time.", "d": "Procedural memory concerns learned skills and habits, which are unaffected by the word-versus-colour conflict of the task."}}, {"q": "Noogenic anxiety is described under:", "concept": "Noogenic anxiety (and noogenic neurosis) is described under the existential school, so the answer is c. The concept comes from Viktor Frankl, the founder of logotherapy, which is often grouped within existential therapy because of its focus on meaning, freedom, and responsibility. Frankl coined the term noogenic, from the Greek noos meaning mind or spirit, to denote disturbances that arise not from psychological conflict in the Freudian sense or from biological causes, but from the spiritual or existential dimension of the person, specifically from a frustration of the will to meaning. Noogenic neurosis and noogenic anxiety stem from an existential vacuum, the experience of emptiness, meaninglessness, and the loss of purpose, and from conflicts of conscience and values rather than from drive conflicts. Frankl argued that the central human motivation is the search for meaning, and that when this striving is thwarted, a distinctively existential form of distress arises that ordinary psychotherapy aimed at drives or symptoms cannot reach; logotherapy addresses it by helping the person discover meaning. The exam discriminator is the link between noogenic, the will to meaning, the existential vacuum, and Frankl, all of which place the concept in the existential school rather than the humanistic, Gestalt, or psychodynamic traditions.", "traps": {"a": "The humanistic school (Rogers, Maslow) emphasises self actualisation and the actualising tendency, but noogenic anxiety is specifically Frankl's existential concept.", "b": "The Gestalt school (Perls) focuses on present awareness and unfinished business, not on noogenic anxiety arising from a frustrated will to meaning.", "d": "The psychodynamic school attributes anxiety to unconscious conflict; noogenic anxiety is defined precisely as not psychogenic in that sense but existential."}}, {"q": "Learned helplessness phenomenon is offered by", "concept": "Learned helplessness is the passivity and failure to escape that follow exposure to aversive events the organism cannot control. It was demonstrated by Martin E. P. Seligman and Steven F. Maier (1967) and by J. Bruce Overmier and Seligman (1967). In the triadic design, dogs in a harness received escapable shock (pressing a panel ended it), inescapable shock yoked to the first group so the amount was identical, or no shock. Later all dogs were placed in a shuttle box where jumping a low barrier ended the shock. Most dogs from the escapable and no-shock groups learned quickly; most yoked dogs lay down and whined, failing to escape. Because shock amount was matched, the deficit came from lack of control, not shock itself. Seligman (1975) proposed this as a model of depression. The original model could not explain why human helplessness is sometimes brief and narrow, sometimes lasting and general, so Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated it in attributional terms: depression follows when bad events are explained by internal, stable and global causes. Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989) extended this into hopelessness theory. Neuroscience led Maier and Seligman (2016) to argue that passivity is the default response to prolonged aversive events and that what is learned is control, detected by the medial prefrontal cortex. Worked example: Rohan, after repeated failure in maths despite effort, stops trying even when a new teacher makes success possible. Option b is right because Seligman and Maier are the researchers who demonstrated learned helplessness.", "traps": {"a": "Aaron T. Beck (1967) built the cognitive model of depression: the negative cognitive triad (negative views of self, world and future), dysfunctional schemas, and cognitive errors such as overgeneralisation and selective abstraction, leading to cognitive therapy and the Beck Depression Inventory. He tempts because Beck and Seligman both offered cognitive accounts of depression and are taught side by side, but Beck worked from clinical observation of patients, not shock experiments with dogs.", "c": "Harry Stack Sullivan, in The Interpersonal Theory of Psychiatry (1953), argued that personality can only be understood through relationships with others. His key ideas include the self-system, which protects against anxiety, personifications, and parataxic distortion (seeing others through the lens of past relationships, close to transference). He tempts because he is a famous name in psychiatry linked to anxiety and mental illness, but he did no experimental research on uncontrollable events.", "d": "Daniel Kahneman, working with Amos Tversky, described judgemental heuristics such as availability, representativeness and anchoring (1974) and prospect theory (1979), which explains loss aversion and framing effects in risky choice; he received the 2002 Nobel Prize in economics. He tempts because his work also concerns how human thinking goes wrong, but he studied biases in judgement and decision-making, not passivity after uncontrollable aversive events."}}, {"q": "Fixed role therapy, which encouraged clients to practice new roles and relationships, was developed by:", "concept": "Fixed role therapy was developed by George Kelly, so the answer is a. George Kelly was the founder of personal construct theory, set out in his 1955 work The Psychology of Personal Constructs. His central idea was that people are like naive scientists who anticipate and make sense of the world through bipolar personal constructs, the contrasting dimensions such as friendly versus hostile that they use to interpret events. Psychological problems arise when a person's construct system is too rigid, too constricted, or fails to predict experience well. Fixed role therapy is the clever therapeutic application of this idea. The therapist, often after a self characterisation sketch written by the client, drafts a fixed role sketch describing a hypothetical character who construes the world somewhat differently, in healthier or more flexible ways. The client is then invited to enact and live out this new role for a set period, as if trying on a different identity in an experiment. By behaving as the new character, the client gathers fresh evidence, loosens fixed constructs, and discovers that alternative ways of construing and acting are possible, which reflects Kelly's founding principle of constructive alternativism, that any event can be construed in more than one way. The discriminating cue, practising new roles and relationships within a constructivist frame, points specifically to Kelly, distinguishing his approach from the cognitive and gestalt therapies of the other options.", "traps": {"b": "Aaron Beck founded cognitive therapy, focusing on identifying and testing automatic thoughts and core beliefs, not on enacting a scripted new role.", "c": "Albert Ellis created Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy, disputing irrational beliefs through the ABC model, a different cognitive method.", "d": "Fritz Perls founded Gestalt therapy, using here and now awareness and techniques like the empty chair, not Kelly's fixed role enactment."}}, {"q": "The most important school of Psychology which has contributed a lot towards perception is:", "concept": "Gestalt psychology is the school that contributed most to the study of perception. Founded in Germany around 1912 by Max Wertheimer, with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka as its other principal figures, Gestalt psychology arose as a direct reaction against the structuralism of Wundt and Titchener, which tried to break consciousness down into elementary sensations. Wertheimer's key demonstration was the phi phenomenon: two stationary lights flashed in quick succession are perceived as a single light moving, an experience that cannot be reduced to the individual sensations and proves that the perceived whole differs from the sum of its parts. From this came the movement's famous dictum that 'the whole is other than the sum of its parts.' Gestalt theorists then formulated the laws of perceptual organization, the principles by which the mind groups sensory elements into coherent wholes: proximity (near things group together), similarity (like things group together), closure (we mentally complete incomplete figures), continuity (we perceive smooth continuous forms), and figure-ground segregation (we separate an object from its background). These laws remain foundational in the psychology of perception today. Because no other school built such a systematic account of how raw sensation becomes organized perception, Gestalt is the correct answer.", "traps": {"a": "Psychoanalysis, Freud's school, concerns unconscious motivation, drives, and personality, contributing little to the experimental study of perceptual organization.", "b": "The behaviorist school, focused on observable stimulus-response learning, deliberately avoided studying internal perceptual processes like organization and grouping.", "c": "The structural school of Wundt and Titchener tried to reduce experience to elemental sensations, the very atomistic approach Gestalt psychology arose to refute, so it is the rival, not the contributor."}}, {"q": "In existential psychology, Dasein encompasses three fundamental psychological dimensions of human existence. Which of the following correctly identifies these three modes of being-in-the-world?", "concept": "The answer is Umwelt, Mitwelt, and Eigenwelt because in existential psychology, particularly as elaborated by Ludwig Binswanger and Medard Boss and popularised for English readers by Rollo May, human existence (Dasein) is understood as simultaneously unfolding across three inseparable modes or dimensions of being-in-the-world. The first is the Umwelt, literally the 'around-world', meaning the physical or natural world of our biology and environment, the world of drives, instincts, and the material surroundings we share with all living things. The second is the Mitwelt, the 'with-world', meaning the social world of our relationships and interactions with other people, the realm of community, love, and human connection. The third is the Eigenwelt, the 'own-world', meaning the private inner world of one's own self, one's relationship to oneself, self-awareness, and personal identity, a dimension existentialists argued is often neglected. A psychologically whole person lives authentically across all three at once, and disturbance can be understood as an imbalance or breakdown in one of them. The trap in this item is that all four options list a famous triad, so you must attach each triad to its owner: Freud's structural model (id, ego, superego), Freud's topographic model (conscious, preconscious, unconscious), Piaget's cognitive stages, and the existential world-modes. Only the German-rooted Umwelt, Mitwelt, and Eigenwelt belong to the existential analysis of Dasein.", "traps": {"a": "Id, Ego, and Superego are the three structures of personality in Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic model, not the existential modes of being-in-the-world.", "c": "Conscious, preconscious, and unconscious are Freud's three levels or topography of the mind, not Binswanger's existential world-dimensions.", "d": "Sensorimotor, preoperational, and concrete operational are three of Jean Piaget's cognitive developmental stages, unrelated to Dasein."}}, {"q": "Which psychologist is associated with the concept of \"affordances\", which refers to the opportunities for action provided by the environment?", "concept": "The correct answer is James J. Gibson. James J. Gibson coined the term affordances to describe the action possibilities that the environment offers an organism: a chair affords sitting, a handle affords grasping, a surface affords walking. Affordances are a cornerstone of his ecological theory of direct perception, which argues that the environment presents rich, invariant information that organisms perceive directly, without the need for internal inference or mental construction. Perception, for Gibson, is geared to action; we perceive what we can do with objects. Hence option B is correct. Hermann von Helmholtz (option A) is associated with the very different idea of unconscious inference, a foundation of the constructivist view that perception is an inferential best guess, which contrasts with Gibson's direct perception. Richard Gregory (option C) was a leading proponent of that constructivist, top-down, hypothesis-testing account, essentially the opposing camp to Gibson's, so he is not linked to affordances. Ulric Neisser (option D) is often called the father of cognitive psychology for his 1967 book Cognitive Psychology, and he later advocated an ecological approach to memory, but the specific concept of affordances belongs to Gibson. The notion of affordances has since become influential well beyond perception, notably in design and human-computer interaction.", "traps": {"a": "Hermann von Helmholtz is linked to unconscious inference in perception, not to affordances.", "c": "Richard Gregory championed the constructivist, inference-based view, the opposite of Gibson's direct perception and affordances.", "d": "Ulric Neisser founded cognitive psychology and later ecological approaches to memory, but affordances is Gibson's concept."}}, {"q": "Who developed the technique of Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT)?", "concept": "The correct answer is Gerald L. Klerman and Myrna M. Weissman. Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) is a structured, time-limited, evidence-based psychotherapy developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (with colleagues) initially for the treatment of major depression. Its central premise is that psychological symptoms, particularly depression, arise and are maintained within an interpersonal context, so improving the person's current relationships and social functioning relieves the symptoms. Rather than focusing on unconscious conflict or on distorted cognitions, IPT concentrates on the here-and-now and targets one or more of four problem areas linked to the onset of the episode: grief (complicated bereavement), interpersonal role disputes (conflicts with significant others), role transitions (major life changes such as divorce, retirement, or a new job), and interpersonal deficits (a history of impoverished or unsatisfying relationships). Its theoretical roots draw on the interpersonal school of Harry Stack Sullivan and on attachment theory from John Bowlby. IPT has strong empirical support for depression and has been adapted for other conditions. The distractors name other clinicians and their distinct therapies: Lorna Smith Benjamin (SASB), Peter Fonagy and Anthony Bateman (mentalization-based treatment), and Otto Kernberg (transference-focused psychotherapy). Because IPT was developed by Klerman and Weissman, option d is correct.", "traps": {"a": "Lorna Smith Benjamin developed the Structural Analysis of Social Behavior (SASB), not IPT.", "b": "Peter Fonagy and Anthony Bateman developed mentalization-based treatment, not interpersonal psychotherapy.", "c": "Otto Kernberg developed transference-focused psychotherapy for personality disorders, not IPT."}}, {"q": "According to Baddeley's Model of Working Memory, the phonological loop is responsible for", "concept": "Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch proposed the multi-component working memory model in 1974 to replace the older single short-term store. It has a central executive that allocates attention, supervised by two subordinate 'slave' systems, with an episodic buffer added later. The phonological loop is the speech-based system: it holds acoustic and verbal information for a few seconds. It has two parts, a phonological store (the 'inner ear' that briefly holds sound-based traces) and an articulatory rehearsal process (the 'inner voice' that refreshes those traces by silent repetition). This is why you can hold a phone number by repeating it to yourself, and why similar-sounding items are harder to recall (the phonological similarity effect). The visuospatial sketchpad, by contrast, handles visual and spatial imagery, and procedural memory belongs to Squire's long-term implicit systems. So the phonological loop is specifically responsible for auditory-verbal material. Remember the mnemonic: loop is for language and sound, sketchpad is for sights and space. Baddeley's model matters clinically because deficits in the loop appear in specific language impairment and some learning disabilities, while executive dysfunction points more to the central executive. Keeping the slave systems straight is a favourite exam distinction, so anchor the loop firmly to the ear and voice.", "traps": {"a": "Visual-spatial information is handled by the visuospatial sketchpad, a separate slave system, not the phonological loop.", "c": "Procedural memory is a long-term, implicit system for skills, not a component of working memory at all.", "d": "Long-term memory consolidation is a hippocampal/systems process distinct from the temporary phonological store."}}, {"q": "The concept of \"self story\" or helping clients re-author their life narratives is most closely associated with which therapeutic approach?", "concept": "The answer is Narrative Therapy because its central, defining idea is that people make sense of their lives through the stories they tell about themselves, and that therapy helps clients re-author or re-story those narratives in more empowering ways. Narrative Therapy was developed in the 1980s by the Australian social worker Michael White and the New Zealand therapist David Epston. Its core assumption is social constructionist: our identities are not fixed inner essences but are constructed through the dominant stories, shaped by culture, family, and language, that we come to believe about ourselves. When a person is dominated by a 'problem-saturated' story ('I am a failure', 'I am an anxious person'), that narrative filters their experience and traps them. The therapist's job is to help the client author a richer, more hopeful alternative story. A signature technique is externalization, captured in the phrase 'the person is not the problem, the problem is the problem', which separates the person from the difficulty (talking about 'the Anxiety' as an external influence rather than an identity) so the client can resist it. Other techniques include finding unique outcomes or exceptions that contradict the problem story, and thickening the new preferred narrative. Because re-authoring the self-story is the hallmark of White and Epston's approach, the answer is Narrative Therapy.", "traps": {"a": "Cognitive Behavioral Therapy works by identifying and restructuring specific dysfunctional thoughts and behaviours, not by re-authoring a client's overarching life story.", "c": "Psychodynamic therapy explores unconscious conflict, defence mechanisms, and transference rooted in early experience, not the deliberate re-writing of one's narrative.", "d": "Gestalt therapy (Fritz Perls) emphasises present-moment awareness, contact, and completing unfinished business, not re-authoring life narratives."}}, {"q": "The capacity of working memory is best-described in terms of number of meaningful units of information, called", "concept": "Working memory (and its predecessor concept, short-term memory) holds a very limited amount of information for immediate use. George A. Miller captured this in his famous 1956 paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two,' proposing that the span of immediate memory is roughly seven items. Crucially, Miller argued that the limiting factor is not the number of raw stimuli but the number of meaningful units, which he called chunks. A chunk is a cluster of elements bound together into a single familiar unit through knowledge and experience. For instance, the string F-B-I-C-I-A is six letters but only two chunks if you recognize the agencies, letting you hold far more raw letters than the seven-item limit would otherwise allow. Chunking is why an expert can recall long strings in their domain: they recode many small elements into fewer high-order units. This reframes capacity as a matter of organization and prior knowledge, not sheer sensory load, and it foreshadows later work showing capacity may be nearer four chunks (Nelson Cowan). The distractors name real cognitive constructs but from other domains: features belong to perception and pattern recognition, scripts and propositions to long-term semantic memory. When a question asks for the meaningful units that define working-memory capacity, the intended term, straight from Miller, is chunks.", "traps": {"b": "Features are elementary perceptual attributes analyzed in pattern recognition, not the units that define working-memory capacity.", "c": "Scripts are schemas for routine event sequences in long-term memory, not the capacity units of working memory.", "d": "A proposition is a unit of meaning in semantic/long-term memory representations, not the term for working-memory span units."}}]});
